// ff8_accessibility.h - Core header for FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod
#pragma once

#include <windows.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>

// ================================================================
// FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod version
// Increment on every build change
// ================================================================
#define FF8OPC_VERSION "0.15.11.0"  // v0.15.11.0: Finish unifying the FF8 text decoders. v0.15.10.0 retired the v0.10.08 standalone decoder (DecodeFF8Char + DecodeFF8String) from battle_tts_helpers.inl and rewrote DecodeFF8String as a thin SEH-safe wrapper around FF8TextDecode::Decode. v0.15.11.0 finishes the job by retiring the third local decoder (DecodeFF8TextPreview in battle_tts_victory.inl) and the small inline ability-name decoder in HookedBtCandidate8. All call sites now route through DecodeFF8String to canonical FF8TextDecode::Decode in ff8_text_decode.cpp — single source of truth across the entire mod. Three coordinated changes: (1) Canonical augmentation in src/ff8_text_decode.cpp + .h: added 0xFA "EC" and 0xFD "FE" to the 0xE8-0xFF compression sequence switch (gaps relative to the preview's v0.13.46 sysfnt.bin token table that had been verified empirically; missing them would have slightly regressed item descriptions containing these compressed pairs); changed 0x0E (icon code) from `return 0` to `return 1` so the icon ID byte is consumed silently instead of leaking into the next decoded char as a spurious character. The preview's small Fire/Magic icon-name table is NOT preserved — it covered only 2 of many possible icons, so the cleaner choice is to lose icon names entirely rather than maintain a partial translation. Both canonical changes are pure improvements with no scan_tts.cpp regression risk (enemy names virtually never contain 0xFA/0xFD/0x0E). (2) Migrated all 7 DecodeFF8TextPreview call sites in src/battle_tts_victory.inl to DecodeFF8String: HookedBtCandidate1 [BTXT] diagnostic + EXP-text strstr capture (matches "Next"/"LEVEL"/"EXP"/"to"/"reach" — pure ASCII, decoded identically by canonical); HookedBtCandidate4 [VAREXP] diagnostic; HookedBtCandidate5 fallback (dropped — canonical handles every case preview did); HookedBtCandidate5 first and second item-description decodes (player-facing for "Received N [item]. Description: ..." TTS); HookedBtCandidate6 entity-name capture; HookedBtCandidate7 fallback (dropped). (3) Replaced the 13-line inline ability decoder in HookedBtCandidate8 with a single DecodeFF8String call. The inline decoder's character table agreed with canonical on 0x21-0x2A digits + 0x2B + 0x31 (the bytes ability names actually use) but disagreed on 0x2E/0x2F/0x30 — bytes that don't appear in real kernel.bin ability-name encodings (the engine uses 0x32 for ability hyphens, which both decoders map to '-'), so the disagreement was effectively dead code and migration is strictly a cleanup. Finally, deleted the DecodeFF8TextPreview function definition entirely from victory.inl and replaced its file-header comment with a unification block. The StripDescriptionTokens helper survives unchanged — it still does useful whitespace/newline normalization on item descriptions; its {XX} stripping branch becomes a no-op because canonical doesn't emit {XX} markers for unknown bytes (it silently skips, matching the v0.13.34 "clean output" behavior preview only partially achieved). Also refreshed the v0.15.10.0 comment block at the top of battle_tts_helpers.inl::DecodeFF8String to reflect the completed unification (was: "the third local decoder in this codebase ... Unification is tracked as a follow-up item in the backlog"; now: "v0.15.11.0: The other two local decoders ... were also retired. Canonical decoder is now the single source of truth across the entire mod."). Predicted v0.15.11.0 BAT: Aaron triggers any battle that produces a victory screen with at least one item drop. ff8_battle.log [BT5-EA30] and [BT6-EA90] log lines decode item / GF / ability names with canonical — if any name was being mangled by the preview's wrong digit range or wrong punctuation it now reads correctly. Item announce TTS "Received N [item name]. Description: [text]" speaks correctly. If a GF level-up or ability-learn event fires in the same battle, [GF-NAME-HOOK] and [ABILITY-NAME-HOOK] log entries still show the correct name + hex; the "GF [name] learned [ability]" TTS still announces (the inline decoder's output for real ability names is bit-for-bit identical to canonical's, so this is verifying the migration didn't break what already worked). The [BTXT] and [VAREXP] diagnostic log entries no longer contain {XX} unknown-byte markers (canonical silently skips); EXP Phase 1/2 announcements unchanged (strstr matches "Next"/"LEVEL"/"EXP"/"to"/"reach" are pure ASCII text that both decoders produced identically anyway). Regression check: scan_tts.cpp enemy-name decoding (in production since v0.14.50, the existing canonical user) still works — it's the same code path now with two additional compression sequences and a tightened 0x0E behavior, neither of which affects enemy names empirically. Risk: medium. Three sources of risk: (a) canonical's 0x0E now consumes a byte where it previously did not — if any enemy name uses 0x0E as a single-byte marker (very unlikely per scan_tts BAT history), the name will be one byte shorter than before. (b) the preview's slightly different punctuation table (0x2E=., 0x2F=-, 0x43=space) had quirks specific to battle-context bytes that we are now assuming don't actually appear; if they do, item names / descriptions will show different punctuation (canonical's 0x2E=!, 0x2F=?, 0x43=apostrophe) — still readable, just different. (c) the inline ability decoder is replaced by a function call — if the call's SEH wrapping triggers a path that differs from inline behavior on malformed pointers, the worst case is the same as DecodeFF8String everywhere else, which is empty output (no regression from the previous inline-decoder behavior on garbage input). Files: src/ff8_text_decode.cpp (compression sequences 0xFA/0xFD added, 0x0E behavior changed), src/ff8_text_decode.h (v0.15.11.0 comment in header doc), src/battle_tts_victory.inl (7 call sites migrated, inline ability decoder replaced, DecodeFF8TextPreview definition deleted, file-header comment rewritten), src/battle_tts_helpers.inl (v0.15.10.0 comment refreshed to reflect completed unification), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.10.2: Three-item cleanup pass, no runtime behavior change. (1) Remove the vestigial WALK_REPRESS_PERIOD constant + s_walkRepressCounter/s_walkRepressLogged statics from src/field_nav_directiondrive.inl. The v0.15.9.7.1 defensive W re-press path was retired in v0.15.9.7.8 (with the InjectKey extended-key fix); the constants were left as vestigial documentation of the history but were unreferenced after .7.8, so this is a pure dead-code strip. The historical narrative explaining the re-press add/remove is also deleted from the .inl; the v0.15.9.7.8 CHANGELOG entry retains the full rationale. (2) Remove the stale FF8OPC_VERSION_DATE macro (was "2026-05-07", manually-maintained, last 10 version bumps did not update it so the DLL init banner read "Version: 0.15.10.1 (2026-05-07)"). Macro deleted from ff8_accessibility.h; banner in src/dinput8.cpp shortened to "Version: %s" using just FF8OPC_VERSION. The Log::Write("Build:   " __DATE__ " " __TIME__) line immediately below already surfaces the real compile timestamp, so removing the manual date macro loses no information. Surfaced by the v0.15.10.1 BAT. (3) Trim BridgeDance log verbosity in src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp::UpdateBridgeDance. The bridge dance state machine shipped in v0.15.9.8.3 with two now-redundant diagnostics: a per-sample "BridgeDance: sample state=X motion=Y..." line firing at 10 Hz (~70 lines per 7s bridge transit), and the LogBridgeDiagnostic all-slots dump function (already early-returned in v0.15.9.11.3.7 but still carrying 200 lines of dead body). Both removed entirely along with the s_bridgeDiagTick counter and its initializers in Initialize() / Disengage(). The transition logs (EAST->WEST, WEST->EAST, WEST->EAST TIMEOUT, leap STARTED) and the "kani read FAILED -- holding state" failure-mode log all stay in place. Files: src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version, FF8OPC_VERSION_DATE removed), src/dinput8.cpp (banner shortened), src/field_nav_directiondrive.inl (walk-repress cleanup), src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (BridgeDance verbosity trim + LogBridgeDiagnostic removal), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Risk: trivial -- all three changes are dead-code/dead-data removal or log-line removal. Worst case (some path we missed still references one of the removed names): build fails with a clear undeclared-identifier error and we add the missing site to the cleanup. Predicted BAT: build_latest.log opens with "Building FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod Version 0.15.10.2", DLL init banner reads "Version: 0.15.10.2" (no parenthesized date), no compile errors, all subsystems initialize as before. Functional regression check: trigger the chase, pick Auto, verify the bridge dance still flips EAST<->WEST cleanly with transition logs but no per-sample spam in ff8_field.log.  // v0.15.10.1: Fix deploy.bat regex regression that printed "Version: SINGLE-PRONGED" in build logs instead of the real version string. Root cause: findstr /C:"#define FF8OPC_VERSION " matched not only line 12 of this header (the real macro) but also the historical v0.15.3 comment at line 63 (a 5,278-char block) which embedded the literal substring "#define FF8OPC_VERSION" while documenting the v0.15.3 fix that tightened the same findstr pattern. for /f's last-iteration-wins behavior left VERSION set to token 3 of line 63: with delims= and the leading-whitespace-indented "  // ..." prefix, tokens parsed as 1=//, 2=v0.15.3:, 3=SINGLE-PRONGED (from "// v0.15.3: SINGLE-PRONGED CLEANUP..."). Beautifully ironic: the v0.15.3 entry's own meta-commentary about its findstr-tightening fix is what re-broke the same regex. Fix: add /B (begin-of-line anchor) to findstr in src/deploy.bat. The real #define is at column 0; all historical mentions are indented "  // ..." so they no longer false-positive. Three #define-prefixed substrings exist in this header overall: line 12 (real macro), line 63 (v0.15.3 historical comment), line 212 (#define FF8OPC_VERSION_DATE) -- only line 12 matches the trailing-space + BOL combined pattern (line 212 has an underscore where the space should be), so VERSION resolves uniquely to "0.15.10.1". No runtime behavior changes; deploy-script-only. Predicted v0.15.10.1 BAT: build_latest.log reads "Building FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod Version 0.15.10.1" at the top and "Version: 0.15.10.1" in the Deployment Complete block, with no "SINGLE-PRONGED" anywhere. DLL identifies itself as Initialized v0.15.10.1 across all subsystems. Risk: trivial -- one /B flag plus comment rewrite, scoped to the deploy script. Files: src/deploy.bat (add /B + comment rewrite), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.10.0: First post-chase backlog work. Fix the X-ATM092 enemy-name decoder bug and consolidate two FF8 text decoders down to one. Battle TTS announced "X-ATM?6?" instead of "X-ATM092" because src/battle_tts_helpers.inl had a standalone v0.10.08 decoder (DecodeFF8Char + DecodeFF8String) whose character table was marked "estimated, not yet confirmed" since v0.10.07. The digit range was off by 0x03 — it mapped 0x24-0x2D → '0'-'9' when the correct Ifrit-canon range is 0x21-0x2A → '0'-'9'. Tracing "X-ATM092" byte-by-byte through the broken decoder produced exactly the observed output: '0' (0x21) outside the wrong range → '?'; '9' (0x2A) inside the wrong range → decoded as '0' + (0x2A - 0x24) = '6'; '2' (0x23) outside the wrong range → '?'. The broken decoder also mismapped 0x06 to apostrophe (Ifrit says color code), 0x2F to '-' (Ifrit says '?'), and had zero coverage of the 24 two-character compression sequences 0xE8-0xFF ("in", "to", "HP", "GF", "ar", etc.) that FF8 packs into kernel.bin to save space — so every monster name using a compressed pair was silently mangled long before X-ATM092 surfaced the issue. Meanwhile src/ff8_text_decode.cpp had the canonical Ifrit-based decoder (FF8TextDecode::Decode) since v04.00, and scan_tts.cpp had been piping the same engine name accessor sub_495100 through it correctly since v0.14.50 across many scanned enemies — empirical proof the canonical decoder works for this exact input source. Fix: replace the body of DecodeFF8String in battle_tts_helpers.inl with a thin SEH-safe wrapper around FF8TextDecode::Decode (split-function pattern lifted from scan_tts.cpp::DecodeNameSafe / DecodeNameToBuf because MSVC /EHsc forbids __try inside a function holding non-trivial destructors per C2712, and std::string inside FF8TextDecode::Decode triggers that). DecodeFF8Char deleted entirely. DecodeFF8String's signature preserved so all five call sites (battle_tts_helpers.inl::GetEnemyName, battle_tts_hp.inl GF-name lookup, battle_tts_menu.inl GF-name lookup, battle_tts_victory.inl HookedBtCandidate5/7 + GetAnyJunctionedGFName + GetGFNameByIndex + VictoryScreenThreadFunc x2) keep working unchanged — only the implementation moved. src/battle_tts.cpp gains `#include "ff8_text_decode.h"` before the battle_tts_helpers.inl textual include so std::string and FF8TextDecode::Decode are in scope. Pre-flight verification: dryRun grep of `DecodeFF8String` and `DecodeFF8Char` across all 18 battle_tts_*.inl files and battle_tts.cpp confirmed exactly three external callers of DecodeFF8String (hp.inl, menu.inl, victory.inl — each one occurrence, all inside __try blocks decoding GF or entity names) and zero direct callers of DecodeFF8Char. Affected enemies beyond X-ATM092: any monster name containing digits (GIM47N, GIM52A, BGH251F2, NORG II) plus any name using compressed pairs (impact wider than the digit-only case but invisible until now because the silent-fail produced subtly-shorter names rather than visible question marks). Caveat to listen for in BAT: the 0x06 case. Old decoder mapped 0x06 to apostrophe; Ifrit says it's a color code that consumes the next byte. If any FF8 enemy or character name actually uses 0x06 for an apostrophe (no canonical example known), post-migration that name will lose its apostrophe AND eat the following byte, sounding one byte short. Character names like "Quistis"/"Squall" decode cleanly using just the letter table, so this is theoretical — worst case is one name reads slightly worse than before, easily patched with a targeted override. Third decoder NOT touched this build: src/battle_tts_victory.inl::DecodeFF8TextPreview has the same digit-range bug but its call sites are mostly diagnostic logging plus item-name announcements (items rarely contain digits). Expanding scope to migrate it too would dramatically widen the BAT surface area; added as backlog item "fully unify all three FF8 text decoders" for a separate session. Predicted v0.15.10.0 BAT: Aaron triggers any battle with X-ATM092 (Dollet harbor chase battles or wherever it first fires). ff8_battle.log shows `[NAME-CACHE] slot3 = "X-ATM092" (base="X-ATM092")` with no '?' and no '6' substitution. `[TARGET] Entry announce: X-ATM092` when targeting the boss. Regression check: a regular-name enemy in any other encounter (Bite Bug, T-Rexaur, Grat, Glacial Eye, etc.) still announces correctly. GF names announced from savemap (Quezacotl, Shiva, Ifrit, etc.) still announce correctly through the hp.inl, menu.inl, and victory.inl paths. Risk: low. The canonical decoder is already battle-tested on identical inputs via scan_tts. Five call sites unchanged because the public function signature is preserved. The SEH-split pattern is proven in scan_tts. If something goes wrong, fallback Option B (one-line patch to the old decoder's digit range) is documented in the prior NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Files: src/battle_tts.cpp (add ff8_text_decode.h include), src/battle_tts_helpers.inl (rewrite DecodeFF8String, delete DecodeFF8Char, replace v0.10.08 comment block), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.9: Fix chase ASK pre-open race window. Aaron reported that pressing confirm during the ~3-second gap between Squall's "Forget it! Let's go!" MES and the chase-mode ASK opening would advance Squall's MES to the chase-start opcode, bypassing the ASK entirely. The 3-second delay was introduced in v0.15.1.2 so NVDA could speak Squall's line cleanly before our ASK preempted, but it created a window where the player's reflex-confirm landed on Squall's dialog instead of the not-yet-open ASK. Fix: lower TRIGGER_DELAY_MS from 3000 to 0 in src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp. The deferred-open check in Update() now resolves on the next AccessibilityThread tick (~16 ms after OnDialogText fires), so the ASK is visible and accepting input before any human reaction time. NVDA's speech queue still sequences the utterances (Squall's line, then the ASK prompt, then the default-cursor option label "Auto: mod drives, no battles or shake") so the audio order is preserved, just without a multi-second silent gap. Default cursor on Auto means a button-mashing player committing without listening still gets the safest option (mod assistance through the chase). The only cost is that a patient player now hears Squall's line under the ASK prompt rather than cleanly before it. If this proves annoying in practice, alternative fixes documented in the code comment: Option A (suppress confirm keys via ChaseKeyboard for just the dialog-settle window) or Option B (gate on ScreenReader::IsSpeaking() instead of a fixed timer). Risk: very low. One-constant edit. Worst case (the ASK and Squall's MES collide in script-VM ways we didn't foresee): revert to a small nonzero delay like 100 ms that still beats human reaction time, or fall back to Option A. Expected v0.15.9.11.3.9 BAT: Aaron triggers the chase, mashes confirm immediately when Squall's line starts. The ASK opens essentially instantly; confirm targets the ASK and commits Auto; chase auto-pilots cleanly through every field as in v0.15.9.11.3.8. Files: src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (TRIGGER_DELAY_MS constant + rationale comment), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.8: Extend chase Auto keyboard suppression beyond arrow keys. v0.15.9.11.3.7 BAT (2026-05-14 20:21-20:25) confirmed the auto-pilot drove the full chase route cleanly (domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> domt2_1 -> domt1_1 -> doopen2a wp 10/27) until a single [CBF] PASS BATTLE fired on doopen2a from `caller=other entityPtr=0x0188CA04` at 20:25:21. JSM scan of doopen2a lists `ent11 cat=3 type=Interactive Object sym='battleyarou'` -- an Interactive Object that triggers a chase BATTLE when the player presses an action key while adjacent. Aaron's report: "All I pressed were arrow keys, though I may have accidentally hit the right CTRL key while hitting arrow keys." Right Ctrl literally borders the right arrow key on standard keyboards. FF8 PC's default keyboard mapping uses Ctrl as one of the action-key bindings, so Aaron's accidental right-Ctrl press reached FF8 (the existing suppression covered only VK_UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT) and fired `battleyarou`'s catch BATTLE. Both `chase_wndproc.cpp::SubclassProc` (WM_KEY* drop) and `dinput8.cpp::HookedGetAsyncKeyState` (VK polling mask) had drop/mask lists restricted to arrow keys -- the comments accurately note all four hooks cover the same arrow keys; nothing covered other action keys. Fix: extend both lists to include the FF8 PC action / menu keys closest to the arrow cluster on a standard keyboard. WndProc drop-list adds VK_CONTROL + VK_RETURN + VK_SPACE + VK_TAB + VK_ESCAPE + VK_SHIFT. GetAsyncKeyState mask-list adds VK_CONTROL + VK_LCONTROL + VK_RCONTROL (explicit left/right so callers polling a specific side -- e.g. the doopen2a chase-progress director -- also get 0) + VK_RETURN + VK_SPACE + VK_TAB + VK_ESCAPE. Both lists exclude VK_SHIFT/VK_LSHIFT/VK_RSHIFT and VK_MENU/VK_LMENU/VK_RMENU from the GetAsyncKeyState mask because the mod's own hotkey handlers in dinput8.cpp poll these via GetAsyncKeyState (Shift+F3/F4 for speech volume; the Alt-gate on every F-key handler via `GetAsyncKeyState(VK_MENU) & 0x8000`). Masking them would silently break those mod hotkeys during chase Auto. WndProc drop-list does drop VK_SHIFT (the mod's GetAsyncKeyState path is independent of WM_KEYDOWN) but leaves VK_MENU alone so Alt+F4 close-window still works during a chase. F-keys and letter keys (Z, X, C, V, A, W, M, O, G, T, L, R, H, digits, /, `, \) stay alone in both hooks -- many overlap with mod hotkeys, and the mod's hotkey poller reads them via the same GetAsyncKeyState path so masking would silently break them. Risk: low. Both hooks already exist and work; this just lengthens their drop-list. The added VK_SHIFT in the WndProc drop-list could in theory affect FF8 / FFNx if either has a Shift-based input handler that consumes WM_KEYDOWN -- mitigated by the mod's GetAsyncKeyState pass-through for Shift (engine-level reads via GetAsyncKeyState still work; only WM_KEYDOWN dispatch is dropped). VK_CONTROL similarly: if any FF8 path consumes Ctrl WM_KEYDOWN for non-chase functionality during chase Auto, that path is briefly suppressed -- acceptable cost during the ~60-second chase window, vs the alternative of `battleyarou` getting triggered on a finger slip. Expected v0.15.9.11.3.8 BAT: Aaron triggers the chase, picks Auto, rides through every chase field WHILE PRESSING ARROW KEYS (and ideally also brushing right Ctrl to specifically test this fix). Field log shows the same install lines as v0.15.9.11.3.7 (ChaseKeyboard ACTIVATED on each chase field, ChaseWndProc subclass already installed at first activate). On doopen2a: 0 [CBF] PASS lines, chase completes to disc00_07h FMV. Verification: re-do the same key-mashing pattern as the v0.15.9.11.3.7 BAT including any inadvertent Ctrl glances; expect zero catches on doopen2a. Files: src/chase_wndproc.cpp (extended VK drop-list with switch statement), src/dinput8.cpp (extended HookedGetAsyncKeyState VK mask-list), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.7: Chase ASK dialog polish post-push (v0.15.9.11.3.6 already on GitHub HEAD 30bc7469). Two small UX changes to the chase mode ASK in src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp: (1) Reorder kChaseChoices[] from {Manual, Auto, Original} to {Auto, Manual, Original} -- now in most-to-least support order. kChaseChoicesDefaultCursor stays 1 but now points to Auto (the new slot 1) instead of Manual. Protects button-mashers: a player who hammers X without listening commits Auto and gets full mod assistance through the chase, not Manual which is the more-demanding option. ANSWER_AUTO and ANSWER_MANUAL swap values (1 <-> 2) to match the new positions; the CommitChoice switch body is unchanged because each case still routes its ANSWER_* to the correct ChaseDetector::MODE_*. (2) Original description rewritten from "Original: vanilla chase, no mod help" to "Original: vanilla, robot keeps getting up to pursue" -- conveys the X-ATM092 vanilla mechanic the previous label didn't capture: the robot rises after each battle and resumes pursuit within the same field, leading to multiple battles per field versus Manual's one-battle-per-field cap. Also fixes a stale comment in the top-of-file design block that referenced the old prompt "Mode?" (replaced in v0.15.9.9) and the old option order. Risk: very low. Three string edits + two constant value swaps. No behavior change to CommitChoice, ChaseDetector::SetChaseMode, DialogInject, or any chase machinery. INI persistence layer stores mode by name ("auto"/"manual"/"original") not by ANSWER_* slot, so existing save files unaffected. Expected v0.15.9.11.3.7 BAT: Aaron triggers chase, ASK announces new prompt + reads the three labels in new order (Auto first, then Manual, then Original), default cursor on Auto. If Aaron confirms without navigating, MODE_AUTO is committed. New Original label is announced naturally during cursor navigation if Aaron arrows down twice to it. Files: src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (kChaseChoices array, ANSWER_* constants, kChaseChoicesDefaultCursor comment, top-of-file design comment, history comment block), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.6: Install a WndProc subclass that drops arrow-key WM_KEY* messages during chase Auto. CLOSES the last untreated keyboard delivery path to FF8's WndProc, after the v0.15.9.11.3.5 BAT confirmed the chase still got caught on doopen2a with arrow keys pressed even with the leak-probe gone -- proving the disruption is not the probe's I/O storm, and not the synthetic-buffer/GetAsyncKeyState paths (already clean per .11.3.4). The v0.15.9.11.3.5 disassembly walk through FF8's WndProc at 0x0040AC5B (the function containing the Ctrl+Q GetAsyncKeyState at 0x0040AE14, confirmed to be a WndProc by structure: switch on uMsg with WM_KEY* branches that route into [+0xb48] per-message-handler dispatch) showed arrow WM_KEYDOWN/WM_KEYUP messages reach a [+0xb48] dispatch table inside FF8's WndProc -- a path the synthetic-buffer GetDeviceState detour, GetAsyncKeyState hook, and v0.15.9.11.3.4 SendInput-collision fix all leave untouched. Either [+0xb48] writes a global the chase-progress director script polls, or the volume of WM_KEY* dispatch on Aaron's keypresses shifts main-thread frame timing relative to the chase director's catch evaluator. Both are addressed by stopping the messages before FF8's WndProc sees them. FIX: new src/chase_wndproc.cpp module with EnsureInstalled() that enumerates top-level visible windows owned by FF8.exe via EnumWindows + GetCurrentProcessId + GetWindowThreadProcessId filter, and SetWindowLongPtrW(GWLP_WNDPROC) subclasses each. The subclass (SubclassProc) runs on whichever thread pumps the message -- for FF8's main game window that's the main thread, the same one that runs the chase script. When ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() AND msg in {WM_KEYDOWN, WM_KEYUP, WM_SYSKEYDOWN, WM_SYSKEYUP} AND wParam in {VK_UP, VK_DOWN, VK_LEFT, VK_RIGHT}: return 0 (the documented WM_KEY* 'handled' reply) without forwarding -- the message never reaches FF8's WndProc, no [+0xb48] dispatch, no main-thread cycles spent on it. Everything else (mouse, paint, timer, non-arrow keys, all messages outside chase Auto) forwards via CallWindowProcW/A so FF8's behavior is unchanged. Character set matched per-window via IsWindowUnicode -> CallWindowProcW vs CallWindowProcA. Permanent install: ChaseKeyboard::Activate() lazy-calls ChaseWndProc::EnsureInstalled() on first chase activation (idempotent on subsequent calls); never uninstalls during gameplay to avoid races against in-flight DispatchMessage on the main thread. Outside chase Auto the subclass is a cheap pass-through (one volatile bool read per message, false short-circuit). NOT WH_KEYBOARD_LL: v0.15.9.11.1/.11.2 used WH_KEYBOARD_LL and the chase broke on doopen2a at wp 5-6/26 ~2s after engagement because WH_KEYBOARD_LL forces every SendInput from any thread to synchronously round-trip through the main thread's message pump -- breaks per-tick path-finding timing. WndProc subclass has no such cost: it runs INLINE in the existing message pump on the existing thread, no extra synchronization. The mod's own SendInput from field_nav_autodrive.inl is already gated on ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() (v0.15.9.11.3.4) -- during chase Auto, the auto-pilot writes directly to the synthetic buffer and skips SendInput entirely, so the subclass only suppresses Aaron's physical keypresses, not the auto-pilot's own arrows. Architecture is now four coordinated hooks during chase Auto: (1) DirectInputCreateA chain -> v7 keyboard device, (2) IDirectInputDeviceA::GetDeviceState vtable detour -> synthetic buffer, (3) user32::GetAsyncKeyState MinHook -> arrow VK queries return 0, (4) WndProc subclass on every top-level window -> arrow WM_KEY* return 0. Together they make every documented and undocumented keyboard delivery path to FF8 return 'no arrow pressed' for the duration of ChaseKeyboard::IsActive(). Expected v0.15.9.11.3.6 BAT: Aaron triggers the chase, picks Auto, rides it WHILE PRESSING ARROW KEYS. If [+0xb48] was the disruptor: full route completes clean (domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> domt2_1 -> domt1_1 -> doopen2a -> dotown_3 -> dotown_2 -> dotown_1), 0 [CBF] PASS lines, matching the v0.15.9.8.3 hands-off result. Mod log near first chase Auto activation shows 'ChaseWndProc: WndProc subclass INSTALLED on HWND 0x...' for each FF8 window discovered. If chase STILL catches on doopen2a with this build, the disruption is happening BELOW the WndProc layer -- raw input messages (WM_INPUT via RegisterRawInputDevices), DirectInput buffered mode rather than immediate state, or some other delivery path we haven't traced -- and next steps are diagnostic (per-message WM type/wParam logging from the subclass to find any arrow-bearing message getting through). Risk: low. WndProc subclassing is a textbook Win32 pattern that has shipped in countless mods/tools/screen readers; SetWindowLongPtrW is documented as cross-thread safe. The CallWindowProc forward keeps FF8's WndProc behavior bit-for-bit identical outside the narrow arrow-key/chase-Auto window. The only fragility is the install-time race (window must exist when EnumWindows runs) which is mitigated by lazy install at Activate (first chase happens many seconds after launch, window definitely exists). Files: src/chase_wndproc.h (new), src/chase_wndproc.cpp (new), src/chase_keyboard.cpp (include chase_wndproc.h, call EnsureInstalled in Activate), src/deploy.bat (add chase_wndproc.cpp to compile list), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.5: Remove the v0.15.9.11.3.3 leak-probe from chase_keyboard.cpp -- it is a confirmed confound. The v0.15.9.11.3.4 BAT (2026-05-13 23:33) proved the .11.3.4 input-layer fix WORKS: the [LEAK-PROBE] lines showed FF8 receiving the synthetic buffer with physical arrows masked (tagged PHYSICAL KEY MASKED on every state change), and the party moved correctly -- waypoints 0-6 reached on doopen2a, running south at normal pace, synthetic buffer coherent. BUT the chase still got caught at wp 6/28 on doopen2a (chase-progress director battleyarou entityPtr=0x0188CA04 fired BATTLE, [CBF] PASS, real catch) -- the SAME failure location as v0.15.9.11.3.1/.2. So across four builds (.11.3.1 through .11.3.4) with the keyboard-device GetDeviceState masking confirmed working, keys-pressed still causes a catch -- meaning Aaron's keys are NOT reaching FF8's movement code (the .11.3.4 disassembly proved movement reads only the masked device buffer), and the disruption is something else that pressing keys triggers. The leak-probe (added .11.3.3, never removed) is now pure liability: HookedGetDeviceState polled the real device an EXTRA time per frame and wrote a [LEAK-PROBE] log line on every physical-key state change -- during key-mashing that is ~20 file-I/O writes/sec on the field thread (and ZERO hands-off). That asymmetric logging load is itself a candidate disruptor of chase Auto timing. Critically: no build has ever run with BOTH the SendInput-collision bug fixed (.11.3.4) AND the probe I/O storm absent -- .11.3.1/.11.3.2 had the SendInput collision, .11.3.3/.11.3.4 have the probe. v0.15.9.11.3.5 removes the probe entirely (HookedGetDeviceState is now just the synthetic-buffer memcpy + return DI_OK; the DIAG_ARROW_DIK array, s_diag* statics, and the Activate() diag-reset are all gone), making this the first genuinely clean test of the .11.3.4 fix. Expected v0.15.9.11.3.5 BAT: Aaron triggers the chase, picks Auto, rides it WHILE PRESSING ARROW KEYS -- if the probe's I/O storm was the remaining disruptor, the chase now completes the full route (domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> domt2_1 -> domt1_1 -> doopen2a -> dotown_3 -> dotown_2 -> dotown_1) clean with 0 [CBF] PASS lines, matching the v0.15.9.8.3 hands-off result. The field log will be MUCH quieter (no [LEAK-PROBE] lines at all). If it STILL catches on doopen2a with keys pressed, the probe is ruled out and the remaining suspect is the Windows message queue -- Aaron's physical keypresses flood FF8's window with WM_KEYDOWN/WM_KEYUP messages, and either FF8's WndProc does something with them beyond the known Ctrl+Q hotkey, or the sheer message-pump volume shifts the main thread's frame timing relative to the chase director's catch clock; next step then is to read FF8's WndProc (function @0x0040AC5B, contains the GetAsyncKeyState call at 0x0040AE14) in the disassembly to see exactly what it does with key messages. Risk: very low. Pure removal of diagnostic-only code; HookedGetDeviceState's substitution behavior (the part FF8 actually sees) is byte-for-byte identical to .11.3.4 -- only the extra real-device poll and the logging are gone. No signature changes, no new dependencies. Files: src/chase_keyboard.cpp (leak-probe removed: comment block, DIAG_ARROW_DIK, s_diag* statics, probe block in HookedGetDeviceState, diag-reset in Activate), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.4: Stop SendInput during chase Auto -- drive FF8 purely through chase_keyboard's synthetic buffer. THE FIX for the v0.15.9.11.3.1/.2/.3 BAT failures (chase completes clean hands-off but gets caught when Aaron presses arrow keys). v0.15.9.11.3.3's leak-probe BAT proved chase_keyboard's GetDeviceState hook IS masking Aaron's physical arrows (Outcome #1: [LEAK-PROBE] lines tagged PHYSICAL KEY MASKED), and the follow-up FFNx + FF8_EN.exe disassembly investigation proved why the chase still broke. FFNx does NOT bridge keyboard into gamepad/joystick anywhere (input.cpp GetGameKeyState only reads the keyboard device; joystick.cpp/gamepad.cpp poll real physical hardware; gamehacks.cpp is the sole consumer and uses them only for FFNx's own speedhack/debug shortcuts). And the FF8_EN.exe input-pipeline disassembly (engine_eval_keyboard_gamepad_input @0x00467D10, get_key_state @0x004685F0, ctrl_keyboard_actions @0x004A2E50, dinput_update_gamepad_status @0x004692B0) proved FF8 reads keyboard state ONLY from the 256-byte DirectInput device buffer whose pointer lives at *0x01CD02D8: engine_eval makes exactly one call per frame to get_keyboard_state (the function FFNx replaces with GetGameKeyState -> keyboard_device->GetDeviceState(256,...), the call chase_keyboard hooks), stores the returned buffer pointer, and every downstream reader (engine_eval's own direct reads, get_key_state's per-scancode accessor, ctrl_keyboard_actions) reads only that buffer. There is NO path in FF8's field-input pipeline that reads OS-level key state -- no GetAsyncKeyState, no GetKeyboardState, no GetKeyState, no WM_KEYDOWN consumption for movement. The lone GetAsyncKeyState import is called from exactly one place (0x0040AE14, inside FF8's WndProc handling WM_KEYUP for the 'Q' key while Ctrl is held) -- a Ctrl+Q hotkey, unrelated to movement, which is why hooking it in v0.15.9.11.3.2 changed nothing. The two DirectInput joystick polls in engine_eval are FF8's own physical joystick (16-byte, dormant -- Aaron has no controller) and dinput_update_gamepad_status (80-byte DIJOYSTATE2, already faked by the mod). CONSEQUENCE: once chase_keyboard substitutes the keyboard device buffer (which the probe proved it does), FF8's keyboard input is entirely the synthetic buffer. The mod's SendInput calls in InjectKey reached NOTHING in FF8 during chase Auto -- they only dumped synthetic key events into the shared OS input queue, where they interleaved with Aaron's physical key events and desynced the diff-based SetHeldDirections model (it tracks what it THINKS is held; Aaron pressing/releasing the same scancodes corrupts that). The bridge dance + keep-alive pulse is the most SendInput-edge-sensitive chase code (deliberate release/re-press every 30 ticks, direction flips on transitions) -- which fits domt1_1 being where v0.15.9.11.3.3 broke. FIX: InjectKey (field_nav_autodrive.inl) now checks ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() FIRST -- when active (chase Auto only), it writes ONLY to the synthetic buffer via SetScancodeDown/SetScancodeUp and returns immediately, skipping SendInput entirely. The synthetic-buffer write is the complete and sufficient delivery path: it produces the same 0x80<->0x00 byte transitions the engine's edge detection (engine_eval_is_button_pressed: confirmed = not(prev & cur) & cur) needs, including for the keep-alive pulse's deliberate KEYUP->KEYDOWN edge. Outside chase Auto (F9 path-finding, world-map AD), ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() is false and InjectKey falls through to the original SendInput path completely unchanged -- non-chase auto-drives keep their existing behavior. ChaseKeyboard is Activated at chase_auto_pilot Engage (before StartDirectionDrive/StartChaseDrive) and Deactivated at Disengage (after StopDirectionDrive/StopChaseDrive), so the entire engaged window -- including StartDirectionDrive's keep-alive pulses and StopDirectionDrive's key releases -- goes through the synthetic-buffer path; the release calls clear the synthetic buffer bytes, then ChaseKeyboard::Deactivate() clears the whole buffer. The previous v0.15.9.11.3 dual-write (SendInput THEN synthetic-buffer write) is replaced by synthetic-buffer-only during chase Auto. Expected v0.15.9.11.3.4 BAT: Aaron triggers the chase, picks Auto, rides it WHILE PRESSING ARROW KEYS -- the party drives through every chase field (domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> domt2_1 -> domt1_1 -> doopen2a -> dotown_3 -> dotown_2 -> dotown_1) without disruption, the bridge dance fires its EAST/WEST transitions cleanly, domt5_1 walks (not runs -- listen for walking-speed audio, since the W-key dual-write delivery wasn't explicitly exercised in a passing .11.3.x BAT), 0 [CBF] PASS lines, chase completes to the Lapin Beach FMV -- matching the v0.15.9.8.3 hands-off result but now with keys pressed. Also worth confirming: no [InjectKey] SendInput FAILED lines (there should be none -- SendInput isn't called during chase Auto anymore). Risk: low. Single-function change, surgically gated on ChaseKeyboard::IsActive(); the synthetic-buffer write path is unchanged from v0.15.9.11.3 (only SendInput is now skipped); no new dependencies (ChaseKeyboard::IsActive/SetScancodeDown/SetScancodeUp were already used in InjectKey). If the chase STILL breaks with keys pressed after this, the next suspect is the W-key synthetic-buffer delivery specifically (the probe only verified the four arrow DIK slots, not DIK_W=0x11) or a timing interaction between the synthetic-buffer write and the engine's per-frame read. If non-chase auto-drives regress, ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() is somehow returning true outside chase Auto -- check Activate/Deactivate call sites. Files: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl (InjectKey: ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() early-return with synthetic-buffer-only write, SendInput path now reached only when chase_keyboard is inactive), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.3: Keyboard-leak probe -- diagnostic-only build to find why physical arrow presses still corrupt chase Auto navigation on doopen2a after v0.15.9.11.3.2's GetAsyncKeyState hook. v0.15.9.11.3.2 BAT (2026-05-13 20:27) confirmed all four keyboard hooks installed (DirectInputCreateA, GetAsyncKeyState, both CreateDevice vtable hooks, GetDeviceState detour) and ChaseKeyboard ACTIVATED fired on doopen2a engage -- yet with Aaron pressing arrow keys the party still got caught at wp 7/28: battleyarou (entityPtr=0x0188CA04) fired BATTLE, [CBF] PASS let it through (AUTO cap=INT_MAX), game-mode 0x0001->0x0003, real catch battle. Same hands-off-vs-keys split as v0.15.9.11.3.1. By static analysis the chain FFNx GetGameKeyState -> keyboard_device->GetDeviceState -> HookedGetDeviceState SHOULD already mask physical keys: HookedGetDeviceState cleanly memcpy's the synthetic buffer and returns DI_OK without polling the real device, and FF8_EN.exe imports only DirectInputCreateA + GetAsyncKeyState for keyboard (both hooked, no GetKeyboardState / GetKeyState / raw input). So the leak is a path static analysis can't see. Aaron's directive: do NOT paper over it with battle suppression -- v0.15.9.8.3 proved the chase completes with zero encounters hands-off, so key presses are corrupting working navigation and the corruption must be found and stopped, not hidden. This build is diagnostic-only: HookedGetDeviceState, when s_active, ALSO polls the real device into a scratch buffer and compares the four arrow DIK slots against the synthetic buffer, logging transition-gated [LEAK-PROBE] lines (first-call marker + state-change events + ~2s heartbeat) so the field log stays readable. Three conclusive outcomes: "state-change ... PHYSICAL KEY MASKED" => the hook IS catching FFNx's reads and IS masking physical arrows, so the leak is a different path and we hunt that next; first-call + heartbeats but physMask never exceeds synthMask => physical arrows aren't reaching the real device while we substitute; no [LEAK-PROBE] logs at all during a chase Auto drive => FFNx is NOT calling this hooked GetDeviceState for field input and we look at FFNx's wiring. Diagnostic state is reset in Activate() so each chase Auto engagement starts clean. Expected v0.15.9.11.3.3 BAT: Aaron triggers the chase, picks Auto, rides it pressing arrow keys, and reads back the [LEAK-PROBE] lines from ff8_field.log -- the doopen2a catch is expected to still happen (this build does not fix anything), but the probe output tells us which of the three outcomes holds and therefore where the leak is. Risk: very low. Pure-additive instrumentation inside the existing hook, gated on s_active (chase Auto only); the extra real-device poll is an immediate-mode read, idempotent and cheap; what FF8 receives is unchanged (still the synthetic buffer). Files: src/chase_keyboard.cpp (LEAK-PROBE in HookedGetDeviceState + reset in Activate), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.2: GetAsyncKeyState hook to close the non-DirectInput keyboard leak path. v0.15.9.11.3.1 BAT (2026-05-13 19:39-19:40) confirmed the DirectInput 7 chase_keyboard hook is installed (startup logs show DirectInputCreateA + IDirectInputA::CreateDevice + IDirectInputDevice::GetDeviceState all hooked) and the GOOD activation message fires on chase Auto engage ("synthetic keyboard buffer now substituted for GetDeviceState reads. Physical key presses will NOT reach the engine"). Suppression worked through domt4_1 / domt3_2 / domt5_1 / domt2_1 / domt1_1 with Aaron pressing arrow keys -- those fields use MODE_DIRECTION / MODE_STAGED / MODE_BRIDGE_DANCE and don't poll keys via the leak path. BUT on doopen2a (first MODE_TARGET path-finding chase field) Aaron's arrow presses started reaching the engine, disrupted the auto-pilot, and triggered the chase-progress director entityPtr=0x0188CA04 to fire BATTLE at waypoint 6/28 -- identical failure pattern to v0.15.9.11.1 / .11.2 (WH_KEYBOARD_LL builds). Test 1 with no keys pressed completed cleanly through doopen2a; only test 2 with keys pressed failed -- the failure correlates with Aaron's physical key presses, not with the field itself, so the leak path must be carrying Aaron's keys to FF8. Root cause: FF8_EN.exe imports GetAsyncKeyState from USER32.dll (FF8_EN_imports.txt line 227). GetAsyncKeyState reads OS-level keyboard state directly, bypassing DirectInput entirely. FFNx's replace_function only replaces FF8's get_keyboard_state -- it doesn't hook GetAsyncKeyState. So FF8's chase-progress director script on doopen2a polls GetAsyncKeyState for arrow keys, sees Aaron's physical presses (which the OS captures regardless of any DirectInput hooking), and fires the catch BATTLE. Earlier chase fields use progression logic that doesn't poll this path. Fix: hook GetAsyncKeyState from user32.dll via MinHook. During ChaseKeyboard::IsActive(), return 0 for VK_UP / VK_DOWN / VK_LEFT / VK_RIGHT queries; pass through all other VK queries to the real implementation so the mod's F-key handlers (F1-F12 voice, F5-F8 audio, F11 screenshot, F12 diag), info-readout keys (V / G / T / L / R / M / H / O), navigation toggles (/ = - \), Backspace GPS, and ` repeat all continue to work normally during chase Auto. The auto-pilot's own arrow presses are NOT consumed via GetAsyncKeyState by any path that matters -- test 1 (no keys pressed) completed the chase successfully with the auto-pilot driving via DirectInput synthetic buffer only -- so returning 0 for ALL arrow queries during chase Auto is safe and simple. The mod's own arrow handler in field_nav_handlekeys.inl is already gated on `s_driveActive && !s_chaseDriveActive` (since v0.15.9.2), so it doesn't query arrow VKs during chase Auto -- no mod-side regression. Expected v0.15.9.11.3.2 BAT outcome: mod log near startup shows new line "DllMain: GetAsyncKeyState hooked at 0x... (from user32.dll). During chase Auto, arrow VK queries return 0; all other VKs pass through. Closes the non-DirectInput keyboard leak path that affected doopen2a in v0.15.9.11.3.1 BAT.". On chase Auto engage: existing ChaseKeyboard ACTIVATED line. Aaron tests with arrow key presses during chase Auto: party drives through ALL fields including doopen2a / dotown_3 / dotown_2 / dotown_1 without disruption, 0 [CBF] PASS lines, chase completes successfully to Lapin Beach FMV. Risk: low. Single new hook on a well-known Win32 API. Hook is gated on ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() so it only affects behavior during chase Auto -- F9 path-finding, world-map AD, all other gameplay unchanged. Graceful degradation if hook install fails (chase Auto still works without arrow suppression, with the v0.15.9.11.3.1 behavior of Aaron's keys leaking through GetAsyncKeyState). If suppression STILL leaves Aaron's keys reaching FF8 after this, the next leak path to investigate is WM_KEYDOWN messages reaching FF8's window proc (unlikely since FFNx's HandleInputEvents consumes them) or some other Win32 API like GetKeyState (not imported by FF8 per the import table, but worth confirming). Files: src/dinput8.cpp (HookedGetAsyncKeyState + InstallGetAsyncKeyStateHook + DllMain wiring), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3.1: Add DirectInputCreateA hook chain for FF8's DirectInput 7 keyboard. v0.15.9.11.3 BAT (2026-05-13 19:05) showed the synthetic-buffer Activate fired but no GetDeviceState detour was installed ("ACTIVATED but hook NOT installed" log line). Aaron's physical arrow presses then disrupted the auto-pilot, confirming keys still reached the engine. Root cause: FF8_EN.exe imports DirectInputCreateA from DINPUT.dll, NOT DirectInput8Create. FF8 uses the original DirectInput 7 API for its keyboard (1999 game, 2013 Steam port preserved the old API). FFNx's input.cpp source confirms: `IDirectInputDeviceA* keyboard_device = *common_externals.keyboard_device` -- old-API interface type. Our IDirectInput8A::CreateDevice hook only catches FFNx's own DirectInput 8 device creations (FFNx uses DirectInput 8 for its own internal polling), not FF8's keyboard. Fix: install a parallel hook chain on the v7 API. (1) Load dinput.dll and MinHook DirectInputCreateA. (2) HookedDirectInputCreateA vtable-hooks IDirectInputA::CreateDevice (vtable[3] on the returned IDirectInputA). (3) HookedCreateDeviceA forwards the returned IDirectInputDeviceA* to chase_keyboard::OnDeviceCreated. Same logic from there: if GUID_SysKeyboard, vtable-hook GetDeviceState (vtable[9]) on the device. The COM vtable layouts of IDirectInputDevice and IDirectInputDevice8 are binary-compatible through GetDeviceState (v8 adds methods at later slots only), so chase_keyboard's existing detour works for both kinds of device pointers via reinterpret_cast at the boundary in HookedCreateDeviceA -- no chase_keyboard changes needed. Also moves MH_Initialize from AccessibilityThread to DllMain (a no-op the second time) so the hook is installed synchronously before FF8's WinMain runs, eliminating any race condition with FF8's init-time DirectInputCreateA call. Expected v0.15.9.11.3.1 BAT outcome: mod log near startup shows both "DirectInputCreateA hooked at 0x..." and "IDirectInputA::CreateDevice hooked at 0x..." lines, then on FF8's keyboard creation "ChaseKeyboard: IDirectInputDevice8A::GetDeviceState hooked at 0x... (vtable[9] on keyboard device 0x...). Synthetic buffer path armed". On chase Auto engage: "ChaseKeyboard: ACTIVATED -- synthetic keyboard buffer now substituted for GetDeviceState reads. Physical key presses will NOT reach the engine". Aaron presses arrow keys during chase Auto: auto-pilot continues uninterrupted. Files: src/dinput8.cpp (DirectInputCreateA hook chain + InstallDirectInputCreateAHook in DllMain + MH_Initialize moved to DllMain), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.11.3: Synthetic keyboard buffer substitution for chase Auto -- the right replacement for the failed WH_KEYBOARD_LL approach in v0.15.9.11.1/.11.2. Architecture: a new chase_keyboard module owns a 256-byte DirectInput keyboard state buffer + a vtable hook on IDirectInputDevice8::GetDeviceState for FF8's keyboard device. When chase Auto is engaged, the hook returns OUR synthetic buffer instead of polling the real device -- so the user's physical key presses never reach FF8 during chase Auto. The chase auto-pilot's InjectKey ALSO writes to the synthetic buffer when ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() is true, so the auto-pilot's arrows + W reach the engine through the same path. Outside chase Auto (F9 path-finding, world-map AD) the hook passes through unchanged -- existing field auto-drive behavior is fully preserved as Aaron requested. Why this works where WH_KEYBOARD_LL didn't: WH_KEYBOARD_LL forced every SendInput call to synchronously round-trip through the main thread's message pump, breaking the chase auto-pilot's per-tick timing (v0.15.9.11.1 / .11.2 BATs both caught on doopen2a at wp 5-6/26 ~2s after engagement). A function-level vtable detour on GetDeviceState has no thread-coordination cost: the auto-pilot writes to a memory buffer, the engine reads it later from the main thread, no SendInput round-trip required for the auto-pilot's keys to be visible to the engine. Discovery path: FFNx's input.cpp has built-in SetBlockKeysFromGame(bool block) that calls memset(keys, 0, 256) when blocked -- this is exactly the right hook point conceptually, but using it directly would also block the auto-pilot's injected keys (no injected-vs-physical distinction at that level). Hooking GetDeviceState one layer deeper lets us substitute the buffer entirely while controlling exactly what the engine reads. Implementation: new src/chase_keyboard.h + chase_keyboard.cpp (the buffer + the GetDeviceState detour, installed via MinHook when IDirectInput8::CreateDevice returns a GUID_SysKeyboard device); src/dinput8.cpp hooks IDirectInput8::CreateDevice in our DirectInput8Create proxy + adds ChaseKeyboard::Shutdown to teardown; src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp calls ChaseKeyboard::Activate() at Engage and Deactivate() at Disengage; src/field_nav_autodrive.inl::InjectKey also writes to the synthetic buffer when ChaseKeyboard::IsActive(); src/field_navigation.cpp includes chase_keyboard.h before its namespace opens so .inl files can reach ::ChaseKeyboard; src/deploy.bat adds chase_keyboard.cpp to the compile list. Graceful degradation: if MH_CreateHook/Enable fails at either CreateDevice or GetDeviceState install, the mod logs a warning and continues -- chase still works without suppression. Scope: chase Auto ONLY. F9 path-finding and world-map auto-drive unchanged. Future scripted-sequence accessibility features (cutscenes, walk-and-talk, timed events) can use the same Activate/Deactivate API; chase Auto is the first user. Expected v0.15.9.11.3 BAT: chase Auto plays through identically to v0.15.9.10 Auto BAT (clean transit, 0 [CBF] PASS, full completion to Lapin Beach FMV). With Aaron pressing arrow keys during the chase, the LL keyboard hook log line is absent (no LL hook this version) BUT chase continues uninterrupted because the synthetic buffer is the only kb state the engine sees. Verification markers: 'ChaseKeyboard: IDirectInputDevice8A::GetDeviceState hooked at 0x... (vtable[9] on keyboard device 0x...). Synthetic buffer path armed' near startup; 'ChaseKeyboard: ACTIVATED -- synthetic keyboard buffer now substituted for GetDeviceState reads. Physical key presses will NOT reach the engine until Deactivate' on chase Auto engage; 'ChaseKeyboard: DEACTIVATED' on disengage. Risk: medium. The CreateDevice + GetDeviceState vtable hooks are new ground for the mod (we previously only used MinHook on FF8 engine functions, not COM interface methods). Both hooks are standard MinHook patterns. If chase Auto still fails on doopen2a, the synthetic buffer write timing vs the engine's keyboard read timing is the next thing to instrument. If non-chase auto-drives regress, ChaseKeyboard::IsActive() somehow returns true outside chase Auto -- check Activate/Deactivate call sites. Files: src/chase_keyboard.h (new), src/chase_keyboard.cpp (new), src/dinput8.cpp (dinput.h include + CreateDevice hook + Shutdown), src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (Activate at Engage + Deactivate at Disengage), src/field_nav_autodrive.inl (InjectKey dual-writes to synthetic buffer), src/field_navigation.cpp (chase_keyboard.h include before namespace), src/deploy.bat (add chase_keyboard.cpp), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.10: MODE_ORIGINAL implementation (Aaron's chase-scene item #2). Adds a real third chase mode that bypasses all mod chase machinery, letting the vanilla FF8 chase play out unmodified. Five-file touch: (1) chase_detector.h: MODE_ORIGINAL=2 added to the Mode enum with rationale comments. (2) chase_detector.cpp: LoadChaseModeFromIni accepts "original" string -> MODE_ORIGINAL; ChaseModeName returns "original" for MODE_ORIGINAL via switch. (3) chase_battle_freeze.cpp Hook_opcode_battle: short-circuits at top of the chase branch -- when mode == MODE_ORIGINAL, return s_origBattle(entityPtr) immediately with no chase-counter increment, no PASS/NO-OP log, no RegisterChaseAgent call. (4) chase_kani_freeze.cpp Update: short-circuits before reading pGameMode; if a freeze was active from a previous mode (mid-chase mode swap via INI), DeactivateFreeze cleanly. RegisterChaseAgent also short-circuits as belt-and-suspenders. (5) chase_ask_overlay.cpp CommitChoice: ANSWER_ORIGINAL routes to SetChaseMode(MODE_ORIGINAL) instead of MODE_MANUAL. chase_auto_pilot needs no change -- its engagement gate already requires mode == MODE_AUTO, so MODE_ORIGINAL naturally skips engagement. Expected v0.15.9.10 BAT: Aaron triggers the chase, ASK reads out the three options, Aaron picks Original. The party experiences the vanilla unmodified chase scene -- chase battles fire as Square shipped them, robot pursues, ground shakes on the west trail, no auto-pilot intervention. Mod's screen-reader assistance (field TTS, etc.) is unaffected. Logs show ChaseBattleFreeze Initialize message includes "MODE_ORIGINAL short-circuits before any chase logic" at startup, and no [CBF] PASS/NO-OP lines on chase fields in MODE_ORIGINAL. Risk: low-medium. Five-file integration but each individual change is small and the short-circuits are at well-defined top-of-hook points. Worst case (a chase machinery path was missed and still activates in MODE_ORIGINAL): the user reports unexpected mod behavior; we add another short-circuit. The mode itself is opt-in via the ASK so default users (MODE_MANUAL / MODE_AUTO) are completely unaffected. Files: src/chase_detector.h (Mode enum + comments), src/chase_detector.cpp (LoadChaseModeFromIni + ChaseModeName switch), src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp (top-of-chase-branch short-circuit + Initialize log update), src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (Update + RegisterChaseAgent short-circuits), src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (ANSWER_ORIGINAL routes to MODE_ORIGINAL), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.9.1: Fix duplicate Squall "Let's go!" line at chase ASK open. v0.15.9.9 BAT confirmed the auto-pilot is fully self-sufficient (0 chase battles fired with cap=INT_MAX) AND the new ASK option labels read out correctly, but Aaron still heard the chase-trigger MES "Forget it! Let's go!" announced twice when the ASK opened. Tracing the dialog log identified the source: field_dialog.cpp's ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows hook (which runs for every opcode_ask) iterates over ALL 8 dialog window slots looking for ASK choice fields. When the chase ASK opens in slot 2, the hook also visits slot 0 (which still holds Squall's chase-trigger MES from 3 seconds earlier). Slot 0's firstQ=0xFF and lastQ=0xFF -- FF8's "no ASK fields set" sentinel -- but the existing skip predicates (firstQ==0 && lastQ==0; lastQ < firstQ) didn't catch the (0xFF, 0xFF) case. The slot got decoded as a 0-choice dialog with a non-empty prompt, then the prompt was spoken as if it were the ASK. Result: "Let's go!" gets announced once by AMESW when the MES fires, then again by the [ASK] hook when the chase ASK opens. v0.15.9.9.1 adds `if (firstQ == 0xFF || lastQ == 0xFF) continue;` to ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows so windows with the FF8 unset sentinel are correctly skipped. 0xFF is unambiguously a sentinel (FF8 dialogs cap at ~16 choices, so neither firstQ nor lastQ can be 0xFF in a real ASK). Risk: very low. One-line additive predicate. Worst case (we missed a legitimate ASK with one of the fields set to 0xFF): the slot's text won't be read out, which is a quieter regression than the duplicate. Files: src/field_dialog.cpp (sentinel check in ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows around line 681), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.9: VERIFICATION BUILD -- combines ASK text polish (item #4) and battle-suppressor disable in Auto mode (item #1). Two coordinated changes: (1) chase_battle_freeze.cpp: AUTO-mode cap raised from 0 to INT_MAX so any chase battle the auto-pilot fails to avoid will PASS through to a real battle screen instead of being silently NO-OP'd. The v0.15.9.8.3 BAT recorded 0 [CBF] NO-OP lines on the whole chase, but that didn't prove whether the underlying chase battle calls were 0 (auto-pilot doing the work) or N>0 (suppressor band-aid doing the work). v0.15.9.9 BAT answers the question: if 0 [CBF] PASS lines appear, the auto-pilot is fully sufficient and the suppressor can be removed in v0.15.10; if any PASS fires, it's a real regression to investigate. (2) chase_ask_overlay.cpp: prompt changed from "Mode?" to "X-ATM092 is heading right for you. How do you want to run?" (replaces the redundant Squall "Let's go!" line at ASK open with situational context). Option labels updated to describe what each does now that Auto is fully implemented: Manual="drive yourself, one battle per field", Auto="mod drives, no battles or shake", Original="vanilla chase, no mod help" (Original still falls back to Manual until v0.15.9.10 implements MODE_ORIGINAL; label change is deferred to that version so we don't promise what the build doesn't deliver). Predicted v0.15.9.9 BAT: ChaseBattleFreeze Initialize log shows "cap=INT_MAX in AUTO (VERIFICATION BUILD)", ASK announces new explainer + new option labels on cursor change, full chase completes with 0 [CBF] PASS and 0 [CBF] NO-OP lines on any chase field. Risk: low. Single-constant change in chase_battle_freeze + 4 string changes in chase_ask_overlay. Worst case (a real chase battle fires): revert cap=INT_MAX -> 0 in v0.15.9.9.1 to restore the band-aid while we diagnose. Files: src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp (cap constant, log message, <climits> include), src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (prompt string, kChaseChoices labels), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.9.8.3: Bridge dance + kani-slot override (domt1_1). Ships TWO coordinated changes targeting the bridge's remaining 1 catch in v0.15.9.8.2 BAT. (1) chase_detector.cpp ApplyPerFieldKaniOverride() runs after the default SYM-name-based kani slot resolution and, on field == 'domt1_1', overrides s_kaniLoc to Others slot 3 (SYM 'laguna'). v0.15.9.8.2 BAT BridgeDiag (416 samples over 6.7 seconds) empirically proved the actually-pursuing entity is at this slot -- ChaseDetector's default 'kani' SYM resolves to Others slot 6 which reads (0,0) the entire transit. The bridge field reuses a generic NPC SYM template for X-ATM092. With the override in place, ReadKaniPosition() and the per-second tick log both read the correct entity for the first time on this field. (2) chase_auto_pilot.cpp adds MODE_BRIDGE_DANCE, a new drive mode for domt1_1 only. Per-tick (10Hz) state machine samples kani.X and classifies motion: LEAPING (deltaX > 200/tick), LANDED (deltaX < 50/tick), CHASING (everything between). Two states: EAST_LEG (initial, drive east) -> turn west when kani lands IN FRONT of party with the wasLeaping latch true; WEST_LEG (drive west) -> turn east the instant a new leap STARTS (mid-air), so party slips past while robot can't course-correct. Aaron's mechanic, verbatim: "Robot jumps east, lands in front of party. Wait for robot to LAND before turning west. ... After turning west, robot eventually jumps again. As soon as the robot jumps (mid-air), turn east immediately -- no wait for landing." Detection signals from v0.15.9.8.2 BAT: kani Y is constant at -446 throughout (jumps are X-axis only in walkmesh coords; visual jump is 3D Z), normal pursuit ~106 units/100ms tick, leap speed ~372 units/100ms tick (3.5x), landed 0-1 units/tick. Thresholds: kBridgeLeapThreshold=200, kBridgeLandThreshold=50, kBridgeLandConsec=2 (samples), kBridgeMinDwellTicks=60 (1s), kBridgeWestTimeoutTicks=300 (5s). The west-leg timeout is the safety net: empirically we don't have data on what the robot does AFTER the party turns west (the v0.15.9.8.2 catch fired before any turn), so we hedge with a 5s bail-back-to-east if no leap fires. Worst-case behavior with timeout firing: identical to v0.15.9.8.2 (1 catch at X~2053 then post-catch teleport advances party). BridgeDiag from v0.15.9.8.2 remains active for one more BAT cycle so we can confirm the slot-3 kani is consistent and capture the previously-missing west-leg robot trajectory. Predicted v0.15.9.8.3 BAT outcome: ENGAGED mode=BRIDGE_DANCE log line on domt1_1, ChaseDetector OVERRIDE log line on field-debounce settle, BridgeDance per-sample logs at 10Hz throughout transit, EAST->WEST transition log after ~6 seconds when laguna lands, then either (best case) WEST->EAST transition on second leap with 0 catches; or (acceptable) WEST->EAST TIMEOUT after 5s and 0-1 catches; or (worst) some unforeseen interaction. Risk: medium. The west-leg detection is inferred from Aaron's verbal description and has no empirical backing yet -- the BAT will give us that data. The east-leg detection is fully calibrated from v0.15.9.8.2 BAT signals. Kani-slot override is surgically scoped to domt1_1; other chase fields keep default ChaseDetector resolution. Files: src/chase_detector.cpp (ApplyPerFieldKaniOverride + call site), src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (MODE_BRIDGE_DANCE enum value, kBridgeXxx constants, BridgeDanceState type, s_bridgeXxx state vars, UpdateBridgeDance helper, Engage/Disengage/Initialize handling, explicit domt1_1 kFieldConfigs entry, ENGAGED log case, IsDirectionLikeMode update, Initialize log message), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.9.8.2: DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY build for the bridge (domt1_1). v0.15.9.8.1 BAT brought total chase catches from 11 (baseline) to 1, with only the mid-east-traversal catch on domt1_1 remaining. Aaron described the bridge mechanic (2026-05-12): the robot jumps east in front of the party, party must wait for it to LAND then turn west, robot eventually jumps again in front (or back over), party must IMMEDIATELY turn east when the jump starts (without waiting for landing); turning prematurely during the east leg causes the robot to run the party down and trigger battle. The asymmetric rule maps to a single Y-axis signal: bridge is a horizontal corridor at Y ~= -607 (confirmed from v0.15.9.8.1 BAT party trajectory), so `abs(kani.Y - party.Y) < tolerance` means "landed on corridor" (east-leg turn condition) and `abs(kani.Y - party.Y) > tolerance` means "airborne / off corridor" (west-leg turn condition). Problem: ChaseDetector currently resolves kani symIdx=12 to Others slot 6 on domt1_1, but that slot reads pos=(0,0) on every per-tick log throughout the v0.15.9.8.1 BAT bridge traversal. Either (a) the bridge uses a different entity for the actively-jumping kani than ChaseDetector's symbol resolution finds, or (b) the resolution is correct but the entity's position is stored at a different offset on this field. v0.15.9.8.2 adds a per-tick (10Hz) diagnostic on domt1_1 that enumerates every non-zero slot of both pFieldStateOthers and pFieldStateBackgrounds, dumping slot index + SYM name + world pos + Y-delta-vs-party for each. BAT analysis identifies the real moving entity; v0.15.9.8.3 then routes ChaseDetector to it and implements the asymmetric dance. **Y-AXIS THEORY REFUTED 2026-05-12**: v0.15.9.8.2 BAT BridgeDiag empirically proved Y is constant on the bridge -- the moving kani at Others slot 3 (SYM 'laguna') has Y=-446 throughout, party has Y=-607 throughout, so ydelta is a useless constant of 161. The jump signal is X-velocity (3.5x normal speed during leap, 0 during landing). v0.15.9.8.3 thresholds were derived from this. No behavior change to the bridge in this build -- chase still runs as in v0.15.9.8.1 with 1 catch. Risk: very low. Pure additive logging, hard-gated to fieldName == "domt1_1". Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (LogBridgeDiagnostic helper + per-tick call + counter), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md.
  // v0.15.9.8.1 [BAT SUCCESS 2026-05-12]: Bump doopen2a targetY from -3703 to -3800 (97 units past the south trigger line at y ~= -3700). v0.15.9.8 BAT showed the south-target fix worked structurally on doopen2a (party ran 3300+ units south in 6s with no NW detour) but A* stopped 159 units short of the trigger line at pos=(-958,-3541) -- likely the last reachable walkmesh waypoint from the requested target (-952,-3703). Party sat there for 8 seconds with chase-drive still active, the chase script's catch evaluator fired once at 23:04:28 forcing 1 [CBF] catch on this field, and the field eventually transitioned at 23:04:34 via the chase script's natural progression after the catch. v0.15.9.8 result: town square went 2 -> 1 catch (-1), bridge unchanged at 1, total chase 3 -> 2 catches. v0.15.9.8.1 hypothesis: by targeting -3800 instead of -3703, A*'s last reachable waypoint should fall closer to or past the trigger line itself, letting the party actually cross the line and fire the script-side field transition before the catch evaluator window expires. If walkmesh extends past the trigger, A* will find a path further south. If walkmesh literally ends at -3541, A* will clip to that same point as v0.15.9.8 and we'll see no improvement -- in which case v0.15.9.8.2 switches to MODE_DIRECTION south (skipping path-finding entirely, like dotown_2 and dotown_1). Single-coordinate edit; risk negligible. Predicted v0.15.9.8.1 BAT outcome (SUCCESS): doopen2a 0 catches, transit 6-8s, clean transition to dotown_3 via south trigger crossing. Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (targetY -3703 -> -3800 + rationale comment), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md.
  // v0.15.9.8: Explicit MODE_TARGET config for doopen2a (Dollet Town Square). v0.15.9.7.8 BAT [CBF] count by field showed 2 catches on doopen2a, root-caused via the field log: doopen2a has ZERO INF gateways, so the BuildFallbackConfig fallback chain dropped to tier 2 (TRIGGER-LINE). The field has two SETLINE trigger lines: idx=1 center (-1390,854) NW and idx=257 center (-952,-3703) SOUTH. GetTriggerLineNearestCluster picked the NW one because the walkmesh's largest dead-end cluster lands at (-1315,503) -- an interior corner. So the auto-pilot targeted NW while the field actually needs the party to run south. v0.15.9.8 added an explicit MODE_TARGET entry for doopen2a targeting the south trigger line center (-952,-3703), bypassing the cluster heuristic entirely. v0.15.9.8 BAT result: 2 -> 1 catches on doopen2a (50% reduction). Remaining issue diagnosed in v0.15.9.8.1.
  // v0.15.9.7.8 [BAT SUCCESS 2026-05-12]: Aaron: "It worked! We successfully made it down the west trail by walking and without triggering the robot." 14 versions of investigation closed. Full chase completed domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> domt2_1 -> domt1_1 -> doopen2a -> dotown_3 -> dotown_2 -> dotown_1. ROOT CAUSE -- InjectKey was setting KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY for all keys. Arrow keys are extended (E0 prefix); letter keys like W are NOT. Every W injection from v0.15.9.7 onward produced malformed scancode E0+11 that FF8's DirectInput keyboard reader didn't recognize as W. So every chase BAT thinking W was pressed had it silently dropped. Arrows worked the whole time (correctly extended); W never worked. Aaron's v0.15.9.7.7 BAT trace (21:58:55 onward) was the smoking gun: pure keyboard path confirmed, override=0 gamepad=0 confirmed, W re-press firing every tick logged -- but party dmag 750-855 (running) instead of 270-303 (walking), and [CBF] catch fired 3 times. Aaron's two-part diagnosis: "I am quite certain it is W. I suspect the W key press is not making its way to the game. You also mentioned that we're repressing W on every tick, but we should be holding W down continuously." Both correct. Fix: (1) InjectKey in field_nav_autodrive.inl gains an 'extended' parameter (default true preserves backward compat for arrow callers); when false, KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY is omitted. world_map.cpp's separate car-control PressKey/ReleaseKey was already fixed in v0.14.102 with the exact same approach; the same fix was just never propagated to field-nav InjectKey. (2) W call sites in field_nav_directiondrive.inl now pass extended=false (3 sites: fresh-start, walk-state flip in already-running, StopDirectionDrive). (3) Defensive W re-press path REMOVED entirely per Aaron's holding-vs-tapping point -- the re-press was added in v0.15.9.7.1 on an unverified theory that the chase intro choreography swallowed the initial KEYDOWN; with the extended-key bug fix, one KEYDOWN should suffice. The OS holds the keyboard state until our explicit KEYUP at Disengage. Re-pressing was potentially counterproductive in DirectInput buffered mode (60 discrete tap events per second instead of one continuous hold). (4) InjectKey now logs SendInput failures (return value != 1) so future dropped-input issues surface in the field log. (5) WALK_REPRESS_PERIOD constant and s_walkRepressCounter/Logged statics remain in the file as vestigial documentation of the history but are no longer referenced. Predicted v0.15.9.7.8 BAT outcome: domt5_1 walks at dmag ~270-303 matching Aaron's manual play. Transit 13-18s. 0 catches. No robot announcement. Field log shows `FRESH-START W KEYDOWN injected (scancode=0x11, extended=0) -- will be held continuously until Disengage (no re-press)` once at engagement, with NO subsequent re-press log lines. If we somehow STILL get a catch after this fix, the issue is genuinely orthogonal to input speed and we need FF8 disasm of the chase script's catch predicate. Risk: very low. The InjectKey signature change is backward-compatible (default extended=true). All existing arrow callers behave identically. Only the 3 W call sites changed. The removed re-press code path was the noisy one; clean removal. Files: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl (InjectKey extended parameter + SendInput failure logging), src/field_nav_directiondrive.inl (W call sites pass extended=false + remove defensive re-press path + comment block rewrite), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.9.7.7: Walk mode skips fake gamepad install + analog override entirely. v0.15.9.7.7 BAT FAILED 2026-05-12: party ran at dmag 750-855, 3 catches. Logs confirmed pure keyboard path was active (override=0, gamepad=0). The W press itself was being silently dropped by KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY bug. Fixed in v0.15.9.7.8.
  // v0.15.9.7.6: Set WALK_ANALOG_MAGNITUDE from 350 to 0 (Aaron's suggestion). v0.15.9.7.6 BAT FAILED: party still ran and triggered the robot. The fake gamepad's PRESENCE (not its analog values) appears to be the issue. v0.15.9.7.7 skips the install entirely for walk fields.
  // v0.15.9.7.5: Reduce analog magnitude when walk=true. Aaron's key insight after v0.15.9.7.4 BAT (2026-05-11/12): FF8 PC walk/run determination depends on input source -- analog path uses deflection magnitude; W modifier doesn't apply. Added WALK_ANALOG_MAGNITUDE = 350. v0.15.9.7.5 BAT FAILED: magnitude 350 still ran and triggered the robot. Reverted to magnitude=0 in v0.15.9.7.6.
  // v0.15.9.7.4: Revert v0.15.9.7.3's Y-axis flip on domt5_1, AND fix domt3_2 direction from east to west. Aaron's 2026-05-11 clarified recipe (verbatim): "The first field, where the chase actually begins, is good to go. The second field, between the starting field and the west trail, you said has the character going east when it should be west, northwest, west. This latest build broke the AD on the west trail. It should be heading generally southwest, south, southeast." Aaron's cardinal-direction terminology (CONFIRMED, document near top of lessons doc): "southwest"=Down+Left=(dirX=-1,dirY=+1) | "south"=Down=(0,+1) | "southeast"=Down+Right=(+1,+1) | "west"=Left=(-1,0) | "northwest"=Up+Left=(-1,-1) | "east"=Right=(+1,0). v0.15.9.7.3 BAT FAILED 2026-05-11: AD got stuck at spawn on domt5_1; Aaron tried to manually move to the next field but couldn't find it. Aaron's earlier "LEFT and slightly UP" message was a description of position-on-the-trail (upper part of the trail toward spawn), NOT screen-direction. I interpreted it as screen-relative and shipped dirY=-1 (screen-up-left) when Aaron's actual recipe is dirY=+1 throughout (SW->S->SE = screen-down-left, down, down-right). Lesson: always translate the player's natural-language description to cardinal directions BEFORE making code changes; document the player's terminology mapping table at the top of the lessons doc for quick reference. See Finding #31 in the lessons doc. v0.15.9.7.4 makes TWO config changes in src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp: (1) domt5_1 reverted to MODE_STAGED_DIRECTION with kStages_domt5_1[] (SW->S->SE staged, walk=true throughout), exactly the v0.15.9.7 / .7.1 / .7.2 config; (2) domt3_2 dirX flipped from +1 (east) to -1 (west), keeping single MODE_DIRECTION; the field is only ~2 seconds in Aaron's manual so single direction should suffice without staging the NW middle. The v0.15.9.5 BAT "success" on domt3_2 (5s / 1 catch) was misattributed in the v0.15.9.5 comment to script-forced kani co-location; we now think it was actually a direction-conflict catch firing in 5 seconds because we were pushing east while Aaron's actual recipe is west. Aaron's manual trace on domt3_2 (2026-05-11 20:14-20:20): 2 seconds of travel, 851 world-units west, no catches. Now the carryover hypothesis from Aaron's earlier pushback ("did you check the possibility if the prior field is somehow carrying over?") becomes testable. If fighting Aaron's manual direction on domt3_2 was priming some script state (kani aggression timer, chase-fighting flag) that carried over to domt5_1 and triggered the catch even with correct direction and walking, then fixing domt3_2 to go west should clear the domt5_1 catch too. v0.15.9.7.4 BAT will verify. If catches still fire on domt5_1 after both fixes, the catch trigger is independent of direction state and the v0.15.9.7.5 investigation focuses on whether SendInput injection vs physical-key input is read differently by the script (would also explain why Aaron's manual run gets 0 catches while v0.15.9.7.2's walking-confirmed run still caught). Both changes are config-table edits only; kStages_domt5_1[] is unchanged from v0.15.9.7; WALK_REPRESS_PERIOD=1 from v0.15.9.7.2 stays in place as defensive coverage. Predicted v0.15.9.7.4 BAT outcome: full chase end-to-end runs cleanly. domt3_2 transit ~2-3s (matching Aaron's manual) with 0 catches (vs v0.15.9.5's 5s/1 catch). domt5_1 transit ~13-18s; ideally 0 catches if carryover hypothesis is right. No AD stuck. Risk: very low; both changes are direction-only in the existing config-table structure. Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (domt3_2 dirX flip + domt5_1 revert + comment trail), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (Finding #31).
  // v0.15.9.7.3: Flip the Y axis on domt5_1 (west trail). v0.15.9.7.2 BAT (2026-05-11, period=1 re-press) confirmed walking reached the engine (audible walking footsteps from first frame) but the robot still triggered. v0.15.9.7.3 INCORRECTLY interpreted Aaron's "LEFT and slightly UP" as screen-up-left (dirX=-1, dirY=-1). v0.15.9.7.3 BAT FAILED: AD stuck at spawn on domt5_1, party couldn't make progress, Aaron couldn't manually find the next field. Reverted in v0.15.9.7.4. The actual recipe per Aaron's 2026-05-11 cardinal-direction clarification is SW->S->SE (down-left, down, down-right) -- the v0.15.9.7 staged config.
  // v0.15.9.7.2: Reduce WALK_REPRESS_PERIOD from 30 to 1 in field_nav_directiondrive.inl. v0.15.9.7.1 BAT (2026-05-11) shipped period=30 and Aaron heard walking on domt5_1 (defensive re-press worked from ~0.5s onwards) but the robot still appeared. Aaron's manual playthrough trace (2026-05-11 20:14-20:20) extracted via new Utilities/dump_chase_events.vbs captured the ground-truth path: every 1-second sample on domt5_1 showed dmag 267-303 (walking speed) from the very first measured frame. He walks the entire field, 13 seconds, 0 catches. Path: (-1004,3253) -> (655,123), net SE throughout, no SW->S->SE transitions -- the field is one direction the whole way in screen-coords (dirX=-1, dirY=+1 which is screen-down-left = world-south-east on this field's inverted-X camera, established v0.15.9.6). Stage transitions in v0.15.9.7 were essentially harmless (walkmesh forces same path) but not what makes it work. Implication for the W-press timing: the engine commits the running-vs-walking decision VERY early on field-load -- likely within the first frame or two after Engage fires. Aaron's physical W press is asserted BEFORE that decision point. v0.15.9.7.1's InjectKey at fresh-start was queued behind the engine's first read OR the script's intro reset keyboard state before the press could land. First-frame keyboard state seen by engine: 'W up' = run. Re-press at t~500ms came too late -- catch flag already set, even though walking-speed motion was audible afterwards. Fix: reduce WALK_REPRESS_PERIOD from 30 to 1. Re-press every tick (~17ms cadence at 60fps). Worst-case latency from field-entry to engine-sees-W-down drops from ~500ms to ~17ms. KEYDOWN on a held key is idempotent at OS level (Windows treats as auto-repeat at input layer; engine re-acknowledges press intent; no glitches). Cost: 60 SendInput calls per second while walking -- SendInput is microsecond-cheap. If the engine reads keyboard at the exact load frame before any SendInput dispatch, even 17ms is too late -- v0.15.9.7.3 would then keep W held across chase-field transitions so the engine never sees a W-up frame on field-load (plumb keepWalkModifier param through StopDirectionDrive, change ChaseAutoPilot::Disengage at field-change to pass true). Risk: very low. One-line constant change. All other behavior unchanged. Other fields (domt4_1, domt3_2, dotown_2, dotown_1, domt2_1, domt1_1, doopen2a) have walk=false; their s_directionDriveWalk stays false so the re-press else-if branch is dead code on them -- no behavior change. Predicted v0.15.9.7.2 BAT outcome: domt5_1 transit ~15-18s with 0 catches matching Aaron's manual. Field log shows [direction-drive] walk modifier RE-PRESSED line within the first second (vs ~0.5s in v0.15.9.7.1). Audio: walking-speed footstep cadence from the first audible footstep. No robot appearance during domt5_1. Bonus utility: Utilities/dump_chase_events.vbs + .ps1 added 2026-05-11 for filtering Logs/ff8_field.log down to chase events only (writes Logs/chase_events_extract.log, ~100KB). Lets Claude read full chase traces in one tool call. Files: src/field_nav_directiondrive.inl, src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (Finding #29), Utilities/dump_chase_events.vbs + .ps1.
  // v0.15.9.7.1: Defensive W (walk modifier) re-press in field_nav_directiondrive.inl's StartDirectionDrive "already running" branch. v0.15.9.7 BAT (2026-05-11 19:35) on domt5_1 navigated the S-curve geometry but the robot caught up because the party was running, not walking -- Aaron's audio confirmed running footsteps and quick robot arrival ("as if we'd run"). Code review confirmed all three kStages_domt5_1[] stages have walk=true and Engage's MODE_STAGED_DIRECTION branch passes stg->walk to StartDirectionDrive correctly, so the W KEYDOWN was fired once via InjectKey in the fresh-start branch. But the diff check (`walk != s_directionDriveWalk`) in the "already running" branch then short-circuited any subsequent re-press for the rest of the field, because both sides stay true. Hypothesis: the chase script's intro choreography on domt5_1 entry (camera pan, mountain shake setup, kani teleport) ran during the first ~1 second and swallowed the SendInput W KEYDOWN before the engine processed it. Once swallowed, our state diverged from engine state forever -- s_directionDriveWalk=true but engine sees W as never pressed -- and the party ran the entire field. Fix: add a defensive re-press path. New constants WALK_REPRESS_PERIOD=30 (matches arrow KEEPALIVE cadence, ~0.5s at 60fps) and state vars s_walkRepressCounter + s_walkRepressLogged. In the "already running" branch's else-if (s_directionDriveWalk) clause (only fires when walk state is already true and didn't just flip), increment counter and fire InjectKey(SC_W_CANCEL_DD, true) every WALK_REPRESS_PERIOD ticks. KEYDOWN on a held key is idempotent at the OS level (Windows treats as auto-repeat at input layer; engine re-acknowledges press intent). NO release between presses -- toggling W mid-walk would cause one frame of run-speed motion between release and re-press (the header comment block already warned about this for the arrow keep-alive pulse, which is why arrows pulse with release+re-press but W does not). The walk-state-flip branch also resets the counter and log latch so transitions between walk and run stages (future MODE_STAGED_DIRECTION fields) work cleanly. Counter and log latch reset on fresh-start and on Stop. Logging: first re-press logs at INFO with the period value, subsequent re-presses suppressed via s_walkRepressLogged latch until next walk-state flip (avoids 60+ log lines per field for the diagnostic). Aaron confirmed only domt5_1 should walk; all other chase fields are walk=false (running) -- config in chase_auto_pilot.cpp already reflects this so this fix touches no other field's behavior. Predicted v0.15.9.7.1 BAT outcome: party walks SW from spawn through first bend, transitions to S at Y=2200, transitions to SE at Y=1100, reaches south exit. Field log shows ENGAGED with `mode=STAGED_DIRECTION walk=1` on domt5_1, then `[direction-drive] walk modifier RE-PRESSED (defensive, period=30 ticks)` once on first re-press. Transit under 18 seconds, target under 6 seconds (Aaron's manual play). 0-2 catches depending on transit time. If party still runs after this fix, the InjectKey path itself is being suppressed -- different root cause requiring SendInput investigation. Risk: very low. Single file changed (field_nav_directiondrive.inl). All other fields have walk=false and never enter the re-press branch (the else-if (s_directionDriveWalk) clause requires walk state to already be true). Walk-state flips still work via the existing diff branch. Files: src/field_nav_directiondrive.inl, src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (Finding #28).
  // v0.15.9.7: Add MODE_STAGED_DIRECTION drive mode and apply it to domt5_1 (west trail). v0.15.9.6 BAT (2026-05-11 18:31-18:34) failed: held south-east at spawn (-1057,3301) drove party into east wall at (-1026,3301) within one second, then froze for ~30 seconds while the script forced bursts of motion that ended at (-751,2325). Party never reached the south exit. Aaron's keyboard recipe confirmed the trail is S-shaped: 'very southwest initially, then go south, then south east at the end' and 'if you press down at first you won't move, you have to press both down and left.' Single-direction MODE_DIRECTION cannot navigate an S-curve -- whatever direction is held is wrong for at least one of the three segments. v0.15.9.7 introduces MODE_STAGED_DIRECTION: a per-field array of FieldStage entries, each with (dirX, dirY, walk, activeMinY). At engagement and every Update tick, PickStageIdx walks the array (in DECREASING activeMinY order) and returns the first stage whose activeMinY <= party Y. When the picked stage differs from the previously-active one, s_engagedDirX/Y/Walk are updated and FieldNavigation::StartDirectionDrive's already-running branch picks up the new analog/arrow values on the next call (diff-based SendInput, see field_nav_directiondrive.inl). No stop/restart needed; keep-alive pulse cycle continues uninterrupted. kStages_domt5_1[] has three stages: SW (dirX=-1 dirY=+1 walk=1) until Y<2200, S (0,+1,1) until Y<1100, SE (+1,+1,1) for the rest. Thresholds derived from BAT evidence: v0.15.9.1.1 BAT showed pure south froze at Y=2217 (trail bends out of south-only corridor there), picked 2200 for SW->S transition. SETLINE call#14 center (-366,1110) marks the final bend toward south-east exit, picked 1100 for S->SE transition. Aaron uses keyboard arrow keys (not analog): Down+Left maps directly to (dirX=-1,dirY=+1) via DD_DirsToArrowMask, so his recipe translates cleanly into stage parameters. Changes: chase_auto_pilot.cpp gets new enum value, FieldStage struct, kStages_domt5_1[] array, FieldConfig.stages/stageCount, MODE_STAGED_DIRECTION branches in Engage()/Disengage()/Update()/LogChaseActiveDiagnostic, IsDirectionLikeMode()/PickStageIdx() helpers, s_engagedStages/StageCount/CurrentIdx state, Initialize() reset and log update. domt5_1 entry switched from MODE_DIRECTION dirX=+1 dirY=+1 to MODE_STAGED_DIRECTION with stages=kStages_domt5_1. Other entries (domt4_1, domt3_2, dotown_2, dotown_1) keep MODE_DIRECTION unchanged but get explicit nullptr/0 for the new stages/stageCount fields. Predicted v0.15.9.7 BAT outcome: party walks SW from spawn through first bend, transitions to S at Y=2200, continues to Y=1100, transitions to SE for final approach to south-east exit. Transit under 18 seconds (catch period is ~6s on this field, so 0-2 catches depending on transit time; <6s = 0 catches; <12s = 1 catch). Aaron's manual target is the under-6s bracket. If a stage's direction still freezes the party at a wall, adjust direction signs or Y threshold for that stage in v0.15.9.7.1 -- stages are isolated, changing one doesn't affect others. Risk: low. Drive mechanism (StartDirectionDrive) unchanged; new code is the stage-selection layer above it. Other fields unaffected. Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp, src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (Finding #27).
  // v0.15.9.6: Switch domt5_1 (west trail) from MODE_TARGET path-finding to MODE_DIRECTION dirX=+1 dirY=+1 walk=1 (WALK SOUTH-EAST). Aaron's recipe for this field (2026-05-11): walk straight through, head directly for next field exit, no hangups, no delay -- robot doesn't catch up if walked straight through; running or delays cause mountain shake / catches. Previous MODE_TARGET with 28 waypoints, velocity-stuck recoveries, and CALIB overhead violated all four requirements except walk, producing 30+ seconds and 3 catches across v0.15.9.4 and v0.15.9.5 BATs. v0.15.9.5 BAT field-log analysis: catches on domt5_1 fire roughly every 6 seconds regardless of party position (kani entity reads (0,0) static, distance-independent) -- this is a TIME-BASED catch trigger, not distance-based. To get to 0 catches, field transit must be under ~6 seconds. Current 32s yields 3 catches; v0.15.9.6 prediction is under 18s with 0-1 catches (best case under 6s with 0 catches matching Aaron's manual play). MODE_DIRECTION satisfies all four requirements: walk=1 satisfies AI rule #1; sustained south-east analog heads directly toward exit; no path-finder state machine means no hangups; no CALIB means no delay. Direction choice dirX=+1 dirY=+1 (screen south-east) based on: (1) world direction from spawn (-1057,3301) to target (382,235) is south-east, (2) v0.15.9.1.1 BAT tried pure-south and froze at (-769,2217) where trail bends east -- south-east adds east component to bypass that wall, (3) CALIB on this field produces non-orthogonal basis (camRight=(0.07,0.998), camDown=(0,-1) -- script motion contaminates calibration) so we can't trust precise camera mapping; south-east is the best heuristic and the BAT validates empirically. Risk: party may freeze at a different wall than pure-south's wall. Fallback in v0.15.9.7 is to try alternate directions (dirX=+1 dirY=0 screen east, dirX=-1 dirY=+1 screen south-west) or revert to MODE_TARGET. v0.15.9.6 changes ONE config entry in kFieldConfigs: domt5_1 from MODE_TARGET tgt=(382,235) walk=1 to MODE_DIRECTION dirX=+1 dirY=+1 walk=1. Plus top-of-file comment block v0.15.9.6 entry, Initialize log message text update. No other source changes. v0.15.9.3 diagnostic instrumentation retained. Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp, src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (Finding #26).
  // v0.15.9.5: Explicit MODE_DIRECTION config for domt3_2 (RUN EAST). **BAT SUCCESSFUL 2026-05-11 18:11-18:14** -- domt3_2 went from 6s/1 catch to 5s/1 catch (-1s from CALIB skip). Catch is script-forced co-location (kani teleported on top of party at field entry; cannot be eliminated without structural change). domt4_1 result reproduced (0 catches/3s, second BAT in a row). Total chase 2:09/7 catches (consistent with v0.15.9.4 2:06/7 within natural BAT variance). Three-signal pattern continues to hold. Pre-BAT rationale follows: v0.15.9.4 BAT was a major success on domt4_1 (3 catches/16s -> 0 catches/3s, validated three-signal direction-selection pattern). Refinement continues to the next chase field in chronological order: domt3_2. Three-signal analysis on v0.15.9.4 BAT data: (1) Camera default. CALIB on this field at 17:28:09 had both phases fail (no movement during the calibration window) but defaults were kept: camRight=(1.000,0.000), camDown=(0.000,-1.000). With defaults, dirX=+1 = world east. (2) Kani co-located with party at engage. First engaged tick showed kani=(-289,-3513), party=(-211,-3461), kdist=93. The chase script teleports both party and kani to the south end of the field on entry, putting them in catch range immediately. No clean flee direction from this signal. (3) Field exit: east. Trigger line endpoints (-71,-3390) and (-139,-3562), nearly vertical, center (-105,-3476). Party engage position (-289,-3513) is well west of the line. After post-catch teleport at (-1358,-3439) is even further west. Both states need east-only motion to cross; the trigger line's verticality means north-south motion doesn't progress toward crossing. Sanity check: pre-engage choreography teleports party from (-367,1139) to (-289,-3513) -- mostly south with negligible east-west bias (+78 east), so the script doesn't impose a preferred east-west direction. Two of three signals point east (camera + exit); kani is null. Plus the CALIB phase 1 and 2 BOTH failed on this field in v0.15.9.4 -- MODE_TARGET wasted ~1 second on a calibration that produced no useful data. MODE_DIRECTION skips CALIB entirely, saving that overhead. v0.15.9.5 adds ONE new entry to kFieldConfigs: domt3_2 MODE_DIRECTION dirX=+1 dirY=0 walk=false (RUN EAST). Plus the top-of-file comment block documenting the analysis and updates the Initialize log message text to reflect current config table. No other source changes. v0.15.9.3's diagnostic instrumentation retained. Predicted v0.15.9.5 BAT outcome: at minimum ~1 second faster transit on domt3_2 (CALIB removed). Possibly 0 catches instead of 1 if party crosses the trigger line before the script forces the co-location catch. If catch still fires, the post-catch teleport puts party further west; direction-drive continues pushing east and party crosses the trigger line. Either way no slower than current behavior. Risk: very low. One new config-array entry. domt3_2's previous behavior (BuildFallbackConfig + INF-gateway/trigger-line fallback) was already doing essentially the same thing in MODE_TARGET; we're switching to a simpler implementation with the same intent. Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp, src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (Finding #25 validates the three-signal pattern with v0.15.9.4 empirical data).
  // v0.15.9.4: domt4_1 direction config changed from RUN WEST to RUN SOUTH-EAST. **BAT SUCCESSFUL 2026-05-11 17:27-17:30** -- domt4_1 went from 3 catches/16s to 0 catches/3s. Total chase 2:06/7 catches (was 2:35/11 catches). Three-signal direction-selection pattern validated empirically; first-second analog motion was dmag=905 (vs dmag=61 on v0.15.9.3 WEST). Pre-BAT rationale follows: v0.15.9.3 BAT diagnostic data on domt4_1 showed the previous WEST direction (inherited from Jegged's strategy guide at v0.15.9.1) was actively wrong: (1) Camera approximately default (camRight=(1,0) camDown=(0,-1) per clean analog sample at sec 9 between catches #2 and #3: analog=(-1000,0) produced world delta=(-61,+6)). So dirX=+1 maps to world east, dirY=+1 to world south. (2) Kani approaches from the north-west of the party at engage moment (kani at (-1645,4154), party at (-657,3132); flee direction is south-east). (3) Field exit is south-east of spawn (party ended at (-404,1117) from spawn near (-1230,3699), net direction south-east). (4) The chase script's own intro animation moves the party south-east during seconds 1-2 of chase activation (pre-engage diagnostic captured delta=(+573,-567)) -- our analog now reinforces the script's flow instead of fighting it. The previous WEST config was the worst possible direction: toward the kani's approach column, away from the field exit, perpendicular to the chase script's natural choreography. v0.15.9.4 changes ONE config entry in kFieldConfigs: domt4_1's dirX from -1 to +1, dirY from 0 to +1. Plus the top-of-file comment block documenting the analysis. No other source changes. v0.15.9.3's diagnostic instrumentation (pre-engage chase-active log + delta computation) is retained -- continues capturing data on this and subsequent chase fields as refinement progresses through domt3_2, domt5_1, etc. Predicted v0.15.9.4 BAT outcome: substantially fewer catches on domt4_1 (target 0-1 vs the 3 catches recorded in v0.15.9.2.18 and v0.15.9.3 BATs). Faster transit to domt3_2 (target <10s vs the 16s recorded). The chase continues normally afterward -- v0.15.9.4 doesn't touch any other field's config or any drive-mode behavior. Risk: very low. One config-array entry edited. Behavior change scoped to one field. Easy to revert to WEST or try other directions if BAT surprises us. Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp, src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (Finding #24).
  // v0.15.9.3: Diagnostic-only build for refining domt4_1 (chase start field). v0.15.9.2.18 BAT completed the chase end-to-end with 11 NO-OPed catches; Aaron's framing: 'our current battle nope system is a band-aid for poor navigation.' Refinement begins with domt4_1 since the chase starts there. v0.15.9.2.18 BAT analysis of domt4_1: 16 seconds total, 3 catches, kani at kdist=401 by tick=60 (1 second after engage). Each catch teleports party slightly south then freezes for 4-5 seconds. Despite analog=lX=-1000 the party drifts net +710 east -2157 south, suggesting the chase script overrides analog input on this field. Two unknowns: (Q1) is analog doing anything at all? (Q2) what's the actual camera orientation? v0.15.9.3 ships two pure-additive diagnostics in chase_auto_pilot.cpp to answer both: (1) Pre-engage chase-active per-second log. New helper LogChaseActiveDiagnostic() called once per second (60 Update ticks) while ChaseDetector reports chase_active=true, regardless of chase_auto_pilot engagement state. Logs party + kani positions and kdist from the moment chase activates (typically on chase-field entry) through the ASK window and into engaged-state ticks. Lets us see where the kani is during the ASK -- data we don't currently have because the existing per-second log only fires when engaged. (2) Movement delta computation. New state vars s_prevPosX/Y/Valid track party position between log lines; the diagnostic computes delta=(dX,dY) dmag=N and includes it in every log line. This is the key data: if dmag is zero or doesn't correlate with our analog direction during engaged ticks, the chase script is in control. If dmag is positive and consistent with (a rotated version of) the analog direction, input is being applied and we can derive camera orientation from the world delta. Output format: ChaseActiveDiag: field='X' state=PRE-ENGAGE|ENGAGED-DIR|ENGAGED-TGT pos=(pX,pY) delta=(dX,dY) dmag=N kani=(kX,kY) kdist=K [analog=(lX,lY)|tgt=(tX,tY)]. After v0.15.9.3 BAT: derive camera orientation from delta during engaged-state ticks where script overrides should be smallest (early in field life before catches start). Cross-check delta direction vs analog direction across all engaged ticks to determine percentage of analog actually applied. Use the data to design v0.15.9.4 (e.g., different direction config, or MODE_TARGET with calibration, or pre-engage analog injection during ASK). BAT plan: load save, walk to chase trigger, commit Auto, hands-off; chase completes (already proven in v0.15.9.2.18); diagnostic logs accumulate. Risk: very low. Two new state vars + one counter + one helper function. No new tools. No behavior change to any drive mode. Existing per-second engaged tick log untouched (parallel diagnostic). Files: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp, src/ff8_accessibility.h, CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.9.2.18: Explicit MODE_DIRECTION config for dotown_1 (mirror of v0.15.9.2.17's dotown_2 fix). v0.15.9.2.17 BAT (2026-05-11 16:11-16:14) ran AUTO mode through the chase. dotown_2 fix worked perfectly: party moved 900 units/sec south via direction-drive, cleared the field in ~14 seconds. But dotown_1 stuck at ~(89,2297) for 24 seconds before disc00_07h.avi finally fired. Field log diagnosis: CALIB phase 1 FAILED on dotown_1 entry (no movement during the 24-tick lX=+1000 test, dist=0.0) -- same symptom as v0.15.9.2.16 BAT but WITHOUT Aaron's manual interference this time, confirming the failure is intrinsic to dotown_1's first-second state. Without proper camera axes, the path-finding drive's analog steering was approximate; party moved ~3300 units in the right direction (spawn (1629,6120) -> ~(89,2297)) then drifted off-course and cycled velocity-stuck / wp-skipping for 24 seconds before eventually drifting into the FMV trigger zone. Aaron's exact observation: 'got right up to the trigger line to the beach fmv, then stopped for some reason before actually triggering it.' Root cause: dotown_1 (like dotown_2) is a Dollet town chase scene where SETLINE-triggered chase scripts dominate; the walkmesh path-finding view of the field is misleading even though A* succeeds. Fix: explicit MODE_DIRECTION dirY=+1 (screen-down) running, mirror of the dotown_2 fix. Direction-mode bypasses CALIB entirely (no camera-axis dependency for screen-relative directions) and never reports arrival (no target), so the party walks continuously south until the field changes via the FMV trigger. v0.15.9.2.17 BAT confirmed default-camera south did move the party in the correct general direction before the drift-off-course problem; MODE_DIRECTION removes the drift mechanism. dirX=0 (no horizontal component) -- same minimal config that worked for dotown_2. If v0.15.9.2.18 BAT shows the party walks south but misses the trigger because it's slightly west of pure south, add dirX=-1 in v0.15.9.2.19. Predicted v0.15.9.2.18 BAT outcome: AUTO mode auto-pilots through the full chase: domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> domt2_1 -> domt1_1 -> doopen2a -> dotown_3 -> FMV disc00_06h -> dotown_2 (direction south, was v0.15.9.2.17) -> dotown_1 (direction south, NEW v0.15.9.2.18) -> disc00_07h.avi (chase climax FMV) fires quickly (within a few seconds of dotown_1 entry, not 30+ like v0.15.9.2.17). CHASE-END SUMMARY mode=auto battles_fired=0 after the FMV terminator field loads. Risk: low. One file changed (chase_auto_pilot.cpp: ~25 lines comment addition + 5-line FieldConfig entry). No API changes, no behavior changes for fields other than dotown_1.
  // v0.15.9.2.17: Explicit MODE_DIRECTION config for dotown_2. v0.15.9.2.16 BAT (2026-05-11 15:21-15:24) showed AUTO mode auto-piloted one field further than v0.15.9.2.15 (reached dotown_3, drove it, completed cleanly with 30 A* waypoints and real arrival at target), then transition FMV disc00_06h played, party loaded on dotown_2, auto-pilot disengaged and stayed disengaged. Field log diagnosis: chase-drive on dotown_2 hit a fragmented walkmesh -- player spawn on tri 45, INF gateway target (-198,-600) on tri 137, trigger-line bridge attempt on tri 77, all on DIFFERENT walkmesh islands. A* found no path (48 iterations, no waypoints). chase-drive STARTED with waypoints=0; calibration's ~500-unit movement triggered the arrival check trivially (no waypoints means 'all waypoints reached' is vacuously true); chase_auto_pilot.Disengage saw reason 'chase-drive completed' and the v0.15.9.2.11 per-field completion marker locked dotown_2 out for the rest of the session. Aaron drove dotown_2 manually for 39 seconds. On the next field (dotown_1), A* found 65 waypoints (workable walkmesh) but calibration failed because Aaron's manual keypresses fought the fake gamepad; auto-pilot couldn't move the party either. Aaron continued manually until disc00_07h (the chase climax FMV) fired. Root cause on dotown_2: Dollet town chase fields use SETLINE-triggered scripted animations to teleport the party between street segments. The walkmesh polygons aren't connected; A* fundamentally can't solve this. Fix: explicit MODE_DIRECTION per-field config for dotown_2 -- direction-mode just pushes the analog south indefinitely, the party walks into the next SETLINE trigger, the engine fires the script, the party transitions. dirY=+1 (screen-down) maps to world south (-Y in +X/+Y screen quadrant) per BAT calibration camDown=(0.291,-0.957). Running (walk=false) -- no AI rule restricts pace on this field; chase is at top speed. dotown_3 and dotown_1 keep the generic INF-gateway fallback (A* worked on both); only dotown_2 needs the override. No general-purpose fix for the 'A* fails -> completion marker traps re-engagement' issue this version -- if Part 2 is needed for future fields, implement separately; Aaron explicitly chose Part 1 only. Predicted v0.15.9.2.17 BAT outcome: AUTO mode auto-pilots through domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> domt2_1 -> domt1_1 -> doopen2a -> dotown_3 -> FMV disc00_06h -> dotown_2 (NEW: direction-drive south) -> dotown_1 -> FMV disc00_07h (chase climax). Auto-pilot should remain engaged through dotown_2 by holding screen-down on the analog; SETLINE scripts teleport the party between segments without A* involvement; eventual transition to dotown_1 disengages on field change and re-engages there with INF-gateway target. CHASE-END SUMMARY mode=auto battles_fired=0 logged after disc00_07h's terminator field loads. Risk: low. One file changed (chase_auto_pilot.cpp: ~25 lines comment block addition + 5-line FieldConfig entry). No API changes, no behavior changes for fields other than dotown_2.
  // v0.15.9.2.16: Extend chase-fields list (CHASE_FIELD_NAMES in chase_detector.cpp) to cover the full chase route through the Lapin Beach FMV. v0.15.9.2.15 BAT drove 6 chase fields end-to-end (domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> domt2_1 -> domt1_1 -> doopen2a) then disengaged on entry to dotown_3 because dotown_3 wasn't in the chase set. The in-game chase continues for 2-3 more fields. v0.15.9.2.15 manual exploration BAT (2026-05-11 14:54-15:01) confirmed the post-bridge route: dotown_3 (Town Square 10, fieldId 0x0158) -> FMV disc00_06h.avi (transition intro, 'ornate car drives through Dollet streets') -> dotown_2 (Town Square 8, fieldId 0x0156) -> dotown_1 (Town Square 6, fieldId 0x0154) -> FMV disc00_07h.avi (the chase climax: X-ATM092 enters town, party flees through streets, robot slams onto bridge, team races across, burst onto beach, Quistis machine-gun cover, landing craft pull away). disc00_07h.avi is the chase terminator FMV. v0.15.9.2.16 re-adds dotown_3/_2/_1 to CHASE_FIELD_NAMES so chase_auto_pilot keeps driving through them; the chase ends naturally when the FMV fires or the field after dotown_1 (not a chase field) is reached. Risk analysis for v0.15.2.11's documented failure mode: v0.15.2.10 hung ~16s after entering dotown_3 because chase_kani_freeze.StartCapture pinned the dotown_3 kani's animation against the in-cutscene script. That failure required a mode 4->1 (battle->field) transition inside dotown_3 to trigger StartCapture. In AUTO mode chase_battle_freeze caps battles at 0 so no battle fires -> no mode 4->1 -> no StartCapture -> no pin -> cutscene plays untouched. In MANUAL mode the cap is 1, but the v0.15.9.2.15 manual exploration BAT walked through dotown_3 without any chase battle firing or hang, suggesting the v0.15.2.10 crash condition isn't reproducible in current builds. Fallback plan if a future MANUAL-mode BAT regresses: gate chase_kani_freeze.StartCapture on a stricter predicate (e.g. IsInKaniActiveChaseField()) while leaving CHASE_FIELD_NAMES inclusive for chase_auto_pilot. Predicted v0.15.9.2.16 BAT outcome: AUTO mode auto-pilots through the full sequence including dotown_3 + dotown_2 + dotown_1, disc00_07h.avi fires from dotown_1, chase ends naturally with CHASE-END SUMMARY mode=auto when ChaseDetector sees the post-FMV field (likely dotown1a or a non-chase field). Battles suppressed will still be non-zero -- the auto-pilot's steering is slow (Finding #21 in Auto-drive lessons), so X-ATM092 will catch the party multiple times during the chase. Each catch is NO-OP'd by cap=0. Steering speed refinement is deferred to a separate version. Bridge trick (AI rule #2: reverse direction when X-ATM092 leaps over party on doopen2a) also deferred -- the v0.15.9.2.15 build doesn't log PJUMPA opcodes or per-tick kani position, so the manual BAT log doesn't contain enough data to reverse-engineer the pattern. A diagnostic-instrumented build is needed for that work, separate task. Risk: low. One source file changed (chase_detector.cpp: 36-line comment block extended, three new entries in CHASE_FIELD_NAMES, doopen2a comment updated). No API surface changes, no signature changes, no behavior changes for fields other than the three additions. Files: src/chase_detector.cpp, src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.9.2.15: Use INF gateway as primary crossing target for chase-drive fallback. v0.15.9.2.14 BAT on domt2_1 confirmed the trigger-line crossing detection logic works, but GetTriggerLineNearestCluster returned FALSE because both SETLINE-defined Line entities on domt2_1 are classified as Event Trigger (kani battle calls 'squall'/'zell'), not Screen Boundary or Unknown -- the filter rejected them. Diagnosis: SETLINE lines are NOT the screen-transition mechanism on chase fields. The INF gateways are. Same BAT field log confirmed the INF gateway parser already runs and emits both gateways: gw[0]=(-93,-3414) destId=321 dest='bgmon_5' (south exit) and gw[1]=(300,1588) destId=328 dest='bgryo1_8' (back where we came from). The engine fires the screen transition when the player crosses gw[0]'s line segment (-497,-3414)->(311,-3414). Aaron's request: 'Let's make sure we're actually targeting the exit gateway and its trigger line.' Fix: (1) FieldArchive::GatewayInfo struct gains lineX1/lineY1/lineX2/lineY2 fields; LoadINFGateways stores the endpoints alongside the center. (2) New public API FieldNavigation::GetGatewayNearestCluster() picks the gateway whose direction-from-player aligns with the player->cluster vector (dot product > 0). This direction test correctly selects forward-progress gateways and rejects entry-back gateways even when entry-back is geometrically closer to the cluster (as on domt2_1: gw[1] is closer to cluster (24+2316 units) than gw[0] (369+2686 units), but gw[1] is opposite to player->cluster direction, dot product -363,763 vs gw[0]'s +10,331,967). (3) StartChaseDrive signature extended with explicit crossLineX1/Y1/X2/Y2 endpoints. When non-zero, copied to new state vars s_driveCrossLineX1/Y1/X2/Y2 and s_driveCrossLineActive=true; UpdateAutoDrive's crossing block reads from these for chase-drive (no longer reads from s_capturedLines via s_driveSkipTrigIdx, because gateways aren't in s_capturedLines). (4) BuildFallbackConfig three-tier preference: GetGatewayNearestCluster -> GetTriggerLineNearestCluster -> GetLargestClusterCenter. (5) Engage passes both trigger index and gateway endpoints; StartChaseDrive picks the one set. Domt2_1 expected outcome: log shows 'INF-GATEWAY line(-497,-3414)->(311,-3414) (cross-product detection)' in fallback config build line. Chase-drive aims 300 units past gateway center (-93,-3414), player crosses gw[0]'s line, 'Arrived.' fires from crossing detection, field transitions to bgmon_5. Risk: medium-high. Seven files changed across multiple subsystems (field_archive.h/.cpp struct change, field_navigation.h/.cpp new API + state vars, field_nav_directiondrive.inl signature change + crossing setup, field_nav_autodrive.inl unified crossing-line read, chase_auto_pilot.cpp fallback logic + passthrough). Existing F9 trigger-line crossing logic preserved (unchanged path for !s_chaseDriveActive). Files: src/field_archive.h, src/field_archive.cpp, src/field_navigation.h, src/field_navigation.cpp, src/field_nav_directiondrive.inl, src/field_nav_autodrive.inl, src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp, src/ff8_accessibility.h, CHANGELOG.md.
  // v0.15.9.2.14: Trigger-line CROSSING detection for chase-drive fallback (not just proximity). v0.15.9.2.13 BAT on domt2_1: tighter arrive distance (60) got the player one more waypoint than v0.15.9.2.12 (wp 16 reached vs only wp 15), but still 'Arrived' while ~300 units from target. Filter rejected lines classified as Event Trigger; v0.15.9.2.15 uses INF gateways instead.
  // v0.15.9.2.13: Tighten chase-drive arrive distance from 300 to 60 units. v0.15.9.2.12 BAT on domt2_1: running footsteps audible, but party Arrived ~300 units from target. v0.15.9.2.13 got one more waypoint but still stopped short; arrive distance is the wrong metric.
  // v0.15.9.2.12: Flip fallback walk default to false (running). v0.15.9.2.11 BAT on domt2_1: mechanically perfect; Aaron heard no footsteps and clarified "the party should be running on this field not walking." Fix: change BuildFallbackConfig walk default to false. domt5_1's explicit config keeps walk=true.
  // v0.15.9.2.11: Stop the engage/arrive/disengage re-engagement loop on chase fields where the fallback target lands inside the player's spawn arrival radius. v0.15.9.2.10 BAT on domt2_1 showed ~60Hz loop: spawn (444,-500), fallback target (276,-727), startDist=282 (inside arrive distance), Arrived. immediately, disengage, re-engage. Hundreds of fake gamepad installs/uninstalls per second. Fix: per-field completion marker. After Disengage with reason "chase-drive completed", mark s_completedField = engaged field. Refuse to re-engage on same field. Clear on field change. Aaron heard footsteps only on west trail (domt5_1) -- separate issue tracked as v0.15.9.2.12 (fallback walk default).
  // v0.15.9.2.10: Re-add ASK gate to chase auto-pilot engagement. v0.15.9.2.9 BAT on domt4_1: Aaron reported "unable to move" on the east trail (chase start), NO footsteps heard the entire BAT. Diagnosis: v0.15.9.1 dropped the !IsAskActive() gate but ignored the 5-12 second window between chase_detector debounce settling and the chase ASK opening. During that window the auto-pilot engaged, installed the fake gamepad, and held arrows + analog through the entire scripted intro -- wedging the input pipeline. Fix: two-step state machine in chase_auto_pilot.cpp (s_askWasActive, s_askAnswered). Engagement requires the ASK to have been observed open AND now closed.
  // v0.15.9.2.9: Two fixes. (1) chase-drive velocity-stuck advance-on-stuck in field_nav_autodrive.inl. v0.15.9.2.8 BAT kani-position diagnostic refuted the collision-push hypothesis on domt2_1: kani sat motionless at (814,-875) for the entire 27-second BAT while Squall moved 1383 units south. Real wp-13 bug: v0.15.9.2.5's advance-on-stuck is nested in the no-progress branch (else of stuckDist < MIN) so it never fires on velocity-stuck (stuckDist ~= 0). Fix: add parallel advance to the if branch. Also fixed IntSqrt (Newton's method needed more iterations for large values; switched to std::sqrt).
  // v0.15.9.2.8: DIAGNOSTIC ONLY. v0.15.9.2.7 BAT (clean logs after spam fix) showed party walked 12 of 18 wps on domt2_1 in ~2 seconds (700 units/sec, faster than calibration's measured walking pace) then froze at (683,8) for 16+ seconds with moveDist=0, NO no-progress-stuck log lines visible (v0.15.9.2.5 advance-on-stuck isn't firing on wp 13 even though conditions hold). Aaron's hypothesis after hearing the BAT: the kani's (X-ATM092 spider's) collision is what pushes Squall through chase fields -- the auto-pilot's analog/kb input may have been doing nothing all along. No footsteps audible on working fields supports this. v0.15.9.2.8 ships a pure-diagnostic ReadKaniPosition() helper that reads the kani's world position via ChaseDetector::GetKaniEntityPtr() (which already caches kani's runtime block at field-change time). The per-second diagnostic log now appends kani=(KX,KY) kdist=<squall-to-kani distance> to every status line. Three patterns to look for: (A) kdist SMALL when wp progress happens and LARGE when stuck -> collision-push confirmed, rethink approach. (B) kdist independent of progress OR party moves with kdist large -> input injection works, wp-13 has a different cause (camera-unreachable funnel wp + advance-on-stuck guard bug). (C) kani=UNRESOLVED on chase fields -> ChaseDetector slot resolution issue. SEH-wrapped pointer read with null-check guard. Helper functions: ReadKaniPosition (reads +0x190/+0x194 from ChaseDetector::GetKaniEntityPtr()), DistSquared (avoids sqrt for thresholds), IntSqrt (Newton's method for human-readable kdist display). Files changed: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (~70 lines: ReadKaniPosition + 2 helpers + per-second diagnostic extension with kaniBuf + header trail entry), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md. Risk: very low. Pure additive diagnostic, no behavior change. SEH wrap protects against pointer faults during field load.
  // v0.15.9.2.7: Fix v0.15.9.2.6 log spam. v0.15.9.2.6 BAT result: ARCHITECTURAL SUCCESS + 2 BUGS. The generic fallback engaged on domt2_1 (no explicit config) and the party walked 12 of 18 waypoints to (276,-727) before getting stuck at wp 13 = (622,-48) for the remainder of the BAT (~16 seconds, position frozen at (683,8), distance to long-range target stuck at 840, moveDist=0). Aaron's chase-multi-field generic engagement request: solved architecturally. But: BuildFallbackConfig() in chase_auto_pilot.cpp was called from Update() at the TOP of every tick (~60Hz), BEFORE the "already engaged on this field" branch. Every call logged "fallback config built for field='domt2_1' mode=TARGET tgt=(276,-727) walk=1 ...", flooding the field log with ~9 KB/sec of identical messages -- ~960 spam lines over 16 seconds of stuck-at-wp-13, with v0.15.9.2.5's advance-on-stuck log lines ("no-progress stuck:" / "chase-drive: skipping wp") presumably drowned out or selectively dropped from the log buffer under pressure. Could not confirm whether advance-on-stuck was firing on wp 13 due to the noise floor. v0.15.9.2.7 fixes the structural bug by reordering Update() so the per-tick refresh path runs FIRST: (1) gate check (wantEngage), (2) fieldName lookup, (3) if engaged on different field disengage, (4) IF ALREADY ENGAGED ON THIS FIELD: per-tick refresh using cached s_engagedX state + diagnostic log + return (no cfg lookup needed). (5) Otherwise (fresh engagement): LookupConfig then BuildFallbackConfig if null then return if both null else Engage. BuildFallbackConfig now fires at most once per fresh engagement on a given field, not once per tick. The per-tick refresh's diagnostic log was also updated to read s_engagedDirX/s_engagedDirY/s_engagedTargetX/s_engagedTargetY/s_engagedWalk (the cached state set by Engage()) instead of cfg->dirX/cfg->dirY/cfg->targetX/cfg->targetY/cfg->walk. Same printed values, no functional difference. Restored quiet logs. Predicted v0.15.9.2.7 BAT outcomes: SUCCESS = clean logs let us see what v0.15.9.2.5's advance-on-stuck does at wp 13 on domt2_1. Three sub-cases: (A) advance-on-stuck WAS firing in v0.15.9.2.6 but logs were lost to spam -- v0.15.9.2.7 logs show it firing, party walks past wp 13, continues through the funnel. (B) advance-on-stuck WAS NOT firing because of some guard we missed -- v0.15.9.2.7 logs show no advance, party stuck same way, refine in v0.15.9.2.8 (probably loosen the no-progress trigger for chase-drive's MODE_TARGET specifically, or fall through to next waypoint when player distance from current wp stabilizes for N seconds regardless of long-range progress). (C) advance-on-stuck fires but it advances into another camera-unreachable wp -- party chain-skips through funnel waypoints until reaching a reachable one. PARTIAL = logs clean but party still stuck, mechanism reveals which guard is blocking. FAIL = something unexpected (build error, regression, etc.). Risk: very low. Code path change is structural only -- same calls, same state, just different order. The s_engagedX cached state was already populated by Engage() before this change, just unused in the per-tick refresh path. Files changed: src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (~30 lines: Update() reorder + diagnostic log uses cached state + header trail entry). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version define). CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.9.2.6: Generic chase-field fallback engagement. v0.15.9.2.5 BAT SUCCESS: party walked the entire west trail (domt5_1) end-to-end with advance-on-stuck firing at wp 7 (camera-unreachable east target) -- Aaron audibly confirmed. After field transition to domt2_1 the party stood still because chase_auto_pilot's per-field config table only knows domt4_1 and domt5_1. ChaseDetector correctly identified domt2_1 as a chase field (chaseField=1, kani symIdx=16, battleyarou symIdx=20), but with no FieldConfig match the Engage() path returned silently. v0.15.9.2.6 ships a generic fallback: when the engagement gate opens (chase field + auto mode + on field) AND LookupConfig() returns null, BuildFallbackConfig() asks FieldNavigation::GetLargestClusterCenter() for the center of the largest cluster found by the walkmesh dead-end scanner at field load (HookedFieldScriptsInit's BFS through 1-2 neighbor triangles). For domt2_1 the scanner found 4 clusters: cluster[1] center=(276,-728) tris=42, cluster[3] center=(-223,-250) tris=10, cluster[2] center=(1495,516) tris=2, cluster[0] center=(-369,-3744) tris=1. Largest is cluster[1] at (276,-728) tris=42 -- almost certainly the south exit corridor (-Y is south on screen). With target (276,-728) and walk=true, F9 path-finder computes an A*+funnel route from the player's starting tri to the nearest tri to (276,-728); chase-drive engages and the party walks the corridor. The same fallback handles every future chase field encountered in the BAT pattern without requiring a per-field code change. Implementation: (1) field_navigation.cpp -- promoted DeadEndCluster struct + s_deadClusters[MAX_DEAD_CLUSTERS]/s_deadClusterCount to file-scope state (was a local variable in HookedFieldScriptsInit, results logged then discarded). (2) field_nav_fieldscripts.inl -- cluster scanner now writes to s_deadClusters; per-field reset block clears s_deadClusterCount. (3) field_navigation.h -- new public API GetLargestClusterCenter(int32_t* outX, int32_t* outY) returns true and fills outX/outY on success, false if walkmesh didn't load or no clusters. Ties broken by first found (lowest seedTri). (4) field_navigation.cpp -- GetLargestClusterCenter implementation (single linear scan over s_deadClusters, max ~32 entries). (5) chase_auto_pilot.cpp -- new BuildFallbackConfig(fieldName) that calls GetLargestClusterCenter and synthesizes a MODE_TARGET FieldConfig in the module-static s_fallbackConfig buffer. Update() now calls BuildFallbackConfig when LookupConfig returns null; if both return null (rare: walkmesh failed to load), behave as before and let the player drive manually. Engage() / Disengage() / per-tick refresh / per-second diagnostic all work unchanged with the synthesized config because they only read cfg fields, not config-source. Initialize log mentions the fallback. Predicted v0.15.9.2.6 BAT outcomes: SUCCESS = same chase trigger flow as v0.15.9.2.5. Auto mode engaged on domt4_1 (DIRECTION run west, explicit config), party walks the field, transitions through it. Engages on domt3_2 if still unconfigured (uses fallback), party heads toward its largest cluster. Engages on domt5_1 (TARGET (382,235), explicit config) -- v0.15.9.2.5 proved this works end-to-end. Transitions to domt2_1, fallback fires with target (276,-728), party walks SE. Either reaches south exit and transitions, or chase-drive completes with stuck. Each subsequent chase field engages via fallback. Chase ends naturally with CHASE-END SUMMARY mode=auto when the chase scene ends. Push v0.15.9.2.6. PARTIAL = fallback engages on domt2_1 but party gets stuck (camera-unreachable funnel waypoints, similar to wp 7 on domt5_1). Advance-on-stuck from v0.15.9.2.5 should handle most cases; if not, refine in v0.15.9.2.7 (perhaps prefer NOT-spawn-cluster, or filter clusters by distance from player spawn). FAIL = fallback fires but the cluster center isn't reachable from player spawn (different walkmesh island, no bridge). Then chase-drive times out and disengages. Rare but possible. Would need a different fallback heuristic (e.g., target an INF gateway center, or scan the walkmesh from the player's tri outward). Risk: low. The fallback only fires when no explicit config matches AND the gate condition is met AND the walkmesh has clusters. All existing per-field configs (domt4_1, domt5_1) are unchanged. F9 path-finding's no-progress branch is unchanged. The new public API is read-only and has no side effects. Files: src/field_navigation.cpp (~22 lines new GetLargestClusterCenter + ~16 lines new DeadEndCluster struct/state), src/field_nav_fieldscripts.inl (~10 lines: use s_deadClusters instead of local + reset), src/field_navigation.h (~20 lines: new API declaration with full doc comment), src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (~50 lines: BuildFallbackConfig + Update integration + header trail entry). src/ff8_accessibility.h. CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md.
  // v0.15.9.2.5: Advance funnel waypoint when chase-drive sees no-progress stuck. v0.15.9.2.4 BAT MAJOR PROGRESS, then SUCCESS in v0.15.9.2.5: kb-from-analog walked 7 wps (1560 units) but stuck at wp 7 (-878,1752) east of player due to rotated-camera X-axis squash (camRight=(0.069,0.998), det=-0.07, lX=+1000 -> only +12 X-world). v0.15.9.2.5 advance-on-stuck workaround: on no-progress detection during chase-drive, advance s_waypointIdx by 1. BAT confirmed party walked full west trail end-to-end. ~22 lines in src/field_nav_autodrive.inl.  // v0.15.9.2.5: Advance funnel waypoint when chase-drive sees no-progress stuck. v0.15.9.2.4 BAT (MAJOR PROGRESS, partial fail at wp 7): the kb-from-analog fix WORKED. Party walked from (-1057,3301) start through 7 waypoints (wp 0 dist=42, wp 1 dist=50, wp 2 dist=49, wp 3 dist=47, wp 4 dist=42, wp 5 dist=47, wp 6 dist=46) reaching (-978,1741) in ~3 seconds -- 1560 units of progress, Aaron heard the party walking. Then stuck oscillating between (-978,1741) and (-978,1770) targeting wp 7 at (-878,1752) for 60+ seconds, dist hovering at 2029-2051 (long-range), dist to wp 7 stuck at ~100. Root cause: rotated camera on domt5_1 squashes X-axis access. camRight=(0.069,0.998) is mostly +Y world; det(camRight,camDown)~=-0.07. Calibration measured lX=+1000 produces only +12 X-world units per phase vs +174 Y-world units -- east movement is ~80x slower than south movement on this camera. wp 7 at (-878,1752) is east of player (need +100 X), but the player oscillates around the wp's Y line (1752) at constant X distance ~100, never advancing east enough to get within the FUNNEL_ARRIVE_DIST=60 threshold. kb tracks analog correctly: when player at Y=1770 (south of wp), analog says NE/screen-up, kb=UR (engine moves player north past 1752 to ~1741); when player at Y=1741 (north of wp), analog says SW/screen-down, kb=DL (engine moves player south past 1752 to ~1770). Classic oscillation around an axis-unreachable target. The no-progress detection IS firing every few seconds ("no-progress stuck: dist=2029 progressBaseline=2051 closed=22 noProgressCount=3 -- forcing recovery"), but recovery is gated off for chase-drive (v0.15.9.2.2 Fix B), so nothing happens. v0.15.9.2.5 fix: in the no-progress branch, when s_chaseDriveActive AND waypoints remaining, advance s_waypointIdx by 1 and reset s_wpMinDist. This treats no-progress as the signal that we've gotten as close to the current wp as the camera will allow, so move on. If the NEXT wp is also camera-unreachable, no-progress fires again, advance again. Eventually we either get to a reachable wp or run out of waypoints (drive times out with default behavior). Predicted v0.15.9.2.5 BAT outcomes: SUCCESS = at wp 7 no-progress detected after ~3 sec stuck, advance to wp 8 = (-803,1659) or (-864,1706) from portal 11 (tri 37->21). From player (-978,1741), wp 8 is +175/-82 east-and-south, both camera-aligned (south is the strong axis). Party walks SE toward wp 8 at full speed, continues along path. PARTIAL = party advances past wp 7 but gets stuck at later wp; logged advance message shows which wp. Refine in v0.15.9.2.6 (perhaps add overshoot threshold relaxation or geometric-tri-id check). FAIL = chain-skip exhausts all 28 waypoints rapidly without movement; means the entire path is unreachable from current camera, would need a completely different navigation approach. Most likely outcome SUCCESS or PARTIAL since we have evidence the kb/analog pipeline works (party walked 1560 units through 6 wps). Files: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl (~22 lines: chase-drive wp advance in no-progress branch). src/ff8_accessibility.h. CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (new finding: funnel waypoints can be camera-unreachable when one world axis is squashed; advance-on-stuck is the workaround). Risk: low. The advance only fires when chase-drive AND no-progress detected AND more waypoints exist. F9 path-finding's no-progress branch behavior is unchanged.
  // v0.15.9.2.4: Derive keyboard heading from analog values for chase-drive (was: long-range target direction via origDx/origDz). v0.15.9.2.3 BAT (PARTIAL FAIL: party still frozen, but with corridor-steering correctly disabled and steer targeting funnel wp). Root cause: kb=DR (long-range SE) vs analog SW = X-axis conflict only, still froze party totally. Any axis-level kb/analog conflict freezes movement; the keyboard's job is to wake up FF8's movement code, not to direct movement -- derive it from analog so the inputs always agree. Files: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl ~40 lines (SetAnalogFromVector moved before heading, dual-branch heading bitmask for chase-drive vs F9).  // v0.15.9.2.4: Derive keyboard heading from ANALOG values for chase-drive (was: long-range target direction via origDx/origDz from v0.15.9.2.2). v0.15.9.2.3 BAT (PARTIAL FAIL: party still frozen at (-989,3195) for 60+ seconds, but with corridor-steering OFF and steer correctly targeting funnel waypoint at (-1225,2811)). The v0.15.9.2.3 fix WORKED -- steer is now the funnel waypoint, not a backward corridor edge. But the party still doesn't move. Periodic log: steer=(-1225,2811) wp=0/28 kb=DR lX=-886 lY=852. Analog projects to mostly south-west on screen (toward funnel wp 0). Keyboard kb=DR is south-east per origDx/origDz from long-range target (382,235). Y axis AGREES (both south). X axis DISAGREES (kb=east vs analog=west). ANY axis-level conflict between keyboard and analog locks FF8's movement -- finding #13 confirmed empirically. Total freeze even with only X-axis conflict. Fix: derive keyboard heading from the analog values (s_analogDesiredLX/LY set by SetAnalogFromVector). Screen-relative: lX > 100 -> DIR_RIGHT, lX < -100 -> DIR_LEFT, lY > 100 -> DIR_DOWN, lY < -100 -> DIR_UP (DirectInput convention: lY +1000 = screen down). Dominant-axis fallback if analog is in deadzone. Keyboard now reflects the screen direction the analog is requesting; both inputs coherent. Restructured code: SetAnalogFromVector now called BEFORE heading bitmask computation (was after) so heading can read s_analogDesiredLX/LY. For F9 path-finding, keep v0.15.9.2.2's origDx/origDz behavior (long-range targets in F9 are usually NPCs far away, so the corridor/funnel turn-point divergence chase-drive hit doesn't typically apply; gated to chase-drive only to minimize F9 regression risk). v0.15.9.2.3 BAT trace (17:25:16-17:26:18 in domt5_1): chase-drive started at (-1057,3301), calibration phase 1 lX=+1000 moved (12,174) producing camRight=(0.069,0.998), phase 2 lY=+1000 moved (0,-230) producing camDown=(0,-1). Player at (-989,3195) post-calibration. Funnel computed 28 waypoints from 30 portal centers; wp 0 = (-1225,2811) which is the L vertex of portal 2 (tri 36->38 edge). This is the correct south-then-east turning point per the corridor route (south to tri 38, then east toward goal). Funnel waypoint targeting was correct. Steering vector also correct (south-west toward wp 0). But the kb=DR vs analog SW perfect-opposition-in-X-axis locked movement. Fix engages keyboard with analog direction: lX=-886 -> heading |= DIR_LEFT; lY=852 -> heading |= DIR_DOWN; heading = DL. Both kb and analog now say south-west. Movement should engage. Predicted v0.15.9.2.4 BAT outcomes: SUCCESS = kb derived from analog produces kb=DL (south-west), party walks SW toward funnel wp 0 at full walking pace, reaches it, wp advances, party continues along the funnel path through the corridor, transitions to next field, chase ends naturally with CHASE-END SUMMARY mode=auto. PARTIAL = party moves but doesn't track waypoints precisely or gets stuck somewhere along the path; refine waypoint advance logic or address whatever else surfaces. FAIL: party still stuck means there's something more fundamental than kb/analog conflict; possibilities include FF8 movement-validation using the engine's stale tri ID to refuse the SW direction (the engine thinks player is on tri 51 north of the portal, so moving SW is fine geometrically, but if engine refuses to leave tri 51 we'd see no movement), walkmesh collision against the corridor's western wall, or some other game-state lockout. If FAIL, v0.15.9.2.5 adds diagnostic: log the geometric tri (computed by mod's own point-in-triangle test) alongside engine tri to confirm or refute the stale-tri-ID-collision hypothesis. Also added new findings to Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (kb-from-analog confirmed, axis-level conflicts confirmed). Files: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl (~40 lines: SetAnalogFromVector moved before heading, dual-branch heading bitmask with chase-drive/F9 split, dominant-axis fallback). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md, Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md. Risk: low. F9's origDx/origDz heading branch unchanged; only chase-drive sees the new logic. Chase-drive itself is exercised only when chase auto-pilot calls StartChaseDrive (other code paths can't trigger it).
  // v0.15.9.2.3: Disable corridor-level steering for chase-drive. v0.15.9.2.2 BAT (PARTIAL FAIL: party fully frozen at (-989,3195) for 60+ seconds). Root cause: corridor steering used engine's stale tri ID (still saying tri 51 even though player geometrically on tri 13), targeted backward 51-13 portal midpoint at (-1135,3293) NW of player. Combined with v0.15.9.2.2 Fix A's kb=DR toward long-range SE target, BOTH axes opposed -> perfect 2D opposition -> total freeze -> engine doesn't reclassify tri -> stale ID persists. v0.15.9.2.3 fix: gate the v06.17 corridor-level steering block on !s_chaseDriveActive. Steer falls back to funnel waypoint (position-based, computed at A* time from portal sequence, no tri ID dependency). Files: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl ~17 lines.  // v0.15.9.2.3: Disable corridor-level steering for chase-drive. v0.15.9.2.2 BAT (PARTIAL FAIL: party fully frozen at (-989,3195) on domt5_1 for 60+ seconds, NO movement at all, kb=DR analog projected to (lX=499,lY=-557) which in world coords is mostly +Y = NORTH on screen, while keyboard DR = south-east. PERFECT 2D opposition, total freeze.) Root cause: corridor-level steering depends on the engine's reported triangle ID (read from entity +0x1FA), which can be STALE. Player position (-989,3195) is geometrically on tri 13 (south of the 51-13 portal at Y=3316-3330), but the engine's tri ID still says tri 51 (the previous triangle). Possibly the engine only reclassifies on actual movement; the player was frozen post-calibration, so the engine never updated. Corridor steering found tri 51 at corridor[0], picked nextTri=corridor[1]=13, and targeted the 51-13 shared edge midpoint at (-1135,3293) -- NORTHWEST of the player. v0.15.9.2.2 Fix A made the keyboard reflect the long-range target direction (SE) via origDx/origDz. So keyboard SE fully opposed analog NW, both X and Y axes. FF8 reads keyboard and analog as voting inputs; perfect opposition = zero movement = engine doesn't reclassify the tri ID = corridor keeps targeting backward portal forever. Chicken-and-egg lockup. Fix A actually made v0.15.9.2.2 WORSE than v0.15.9.2.1: in v0.15.9.2.1 the DIR_UP fallback fought analog SW in Y axis only (X axis free so player drifted), in v0.15.9.2.2 kb=DR fully opposed analog NW so player completely frozen. The correct fix is to not depend on the engine's per-tick tri ID for chase-drive steering. v0.15.9.2.3 ships ONE change: gate the v06.17 corridor-level steering block on `!s_chaseDriveActive`. When chase-drive is active, the steer target is the funnel waypoint at s_waypoints[s_waypointIdx] (computed by FunnelPath at A* time from the portal sequence, position-based, not per-tick-tri-ID-based). Funnel waypoints can be slightly less precise at edge crossings (the corridor steering's job was to anchor the player to specific portal midpoints), but they don't suffer from the stale-tri-ID feedback loop. The funnel waypoint advances naturally as the player closes on it (FUNNEL_ARRIVE_DIST=60 trigger plus overshoot detection), pulling the player along the planned path. Predicted v0.15.9.2.3 BAT outcomes: SUCCESS = with corridor steering off, the steer target becomes funnel waypoint 0 (somewhere along the south-tending corridor). Keyboard kb=DR (toward long-range SE target) and analog (toward funnel wp 0) should largely agree -- the funnel waypoints follow the corridor which goes south then east, so wp 0 is probably somewhere south of player. Party walks south at calibration-pace, eventually reaches funnel waypoints further along, crosses through the south exit gateway, chase ends naturally. PARTIAL = party moves but doesn't reach exit because funnel waypoints aren't tracked precisely (the corridor was supposed to fix that). Refine in v0.15.9.2.4. FAIL = party still stuck means there's another issue we haven't identified (e.g., walkmesh collision against tri 13's geometry from the south side, FF8 movement-validation refusing to enter tri 13 because engine still thinks player is in tri 51, etc.). If FAIL, debug with diagnostic: log player's actual geometric tri (computed locally via point-in-triangle test) alongside engine's reported tri, identify divergence. Also added three new findings to Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (engine tri ID staleness, funnel-vs-corridor reliability, perfect-opposition lockup). Files changed: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl (~17 lines: one condition + rationale comment block). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Plan & Research Documents/Auto-drive lessons from chase auto-pilot.md (3 new findings, retitled priorities). Risk: low. Corridor steering remains active for F9 path-finding (untouched, gated only when s_chaseDriveActive is true which only chase auto-pilot sets). Chase-drive's funnel waypoints are the same code path F9 uses when corridor steering is off in normal cases (e.g., near goal, dist < threshold).
  // v0.15.9.2.2: Three coordinated fixes for v0.15.9.2.1 BAT (PARTIAL FAIL: party stuck thrashing near spawn on domt5_1, oscillating between tri 29 and tri 32 within a 30-unit radius for 20+ seconds, footsteps audible throughout per Aaron). Fix A: heading bitmask uses origDx/origDz (long-range target direction) instead of corridor-overwritten dx/dz, so keyboard wake-up trigger always reflects the broad target direction. Fix B: skip stuck-detection-and-recovery block for chase-drive (recovery's perp-nudge logic is wrong for elongated triangles on rotated cameras). Fix C: extended timeout 12000 ticks (200s) for chase-drive. Files: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl ~50 lines.  // v0.15.9.2.2: Three coordinated fixes for v0.15.9.2.1 BAT (PARTIAL FAIL: party stuck thrashing near spawn on domt5_1, oscillating between tri 29 and tri 32 within a 30-unit radius for 20+ seconds, footsteps audible throughout per Aaron). Field log analysis revealed THREE distinct problems: (1) HEADING-FALLBACK BUG -- corridor-level steering overwrites dx/dz to point at the nearby shared-edge midpoint between current and next corridor triangle. On domt5_1 the midpoint is only ~30 units west and ~116 units south of the player, both below DRIVE_AXIS_THRESH=150. The heading-bitmask threshold check fails for ALL four direction bits, and the fallback `if (heading == 0) heading = DIR_UP;` fires. Keyboard presses UP arrow every tick while the analog says SW (toward the actual target). FF8 reads keyboard and analog as voting inputs; with them in DIRECT OPPOSITION, the engine resolves to crawl-speed. Periodic [drive] logs show kb=U (UP only) for 20 seconds straight despite analog lX=-950 lY=+936. Result: 30 units of movement in 2-second main-steering windows = 15 units/sec walk pace, 50x slower than calibration's 864 units/sec. (2) MISDIRECTED RECOVERY NUDGES -- every ~1.5 seconds DRIVE_STUCK_THRESH=80 ticks fires recovery. Even phases pick perpendicular nudge direction whose dot-product with (next-tri-center - player) is larger; this prefers the perp pointing TOWARD the centroid. On elongated triangles (tri 51 in domt5_1 spans X from -1313 to ~-980, ~330 units wide), the centroid can be EAST of the player even when the shared edge to cross is WEST. The chosen perp pushes AWAY from the edge the corridor wants to cross. Worse: on rotated-camera fields like domt5_1 (camRight=(0.041,0.999), camDown=(0,-1) -- both axes nearly parallel to world +Y/-Y, cross-product determinant ~-0.041), the world-direction nudge (1.0, -0.01) projects through SetAnalogFromVector to lX=31 lY=10 -- tiny values the engine treats as deadzone analog. Net result: nudge produces no useful movement but the small lY=10 alone projects back to slight +Y world, drifting the player NORTH into tri 29. Next recovery fires from tri 29 wanting south back to tri 32. Player oscillates between tri 29 and tri 32 forever. (3) TOO-SHORT TIMEOUT -- DRIVE_MAX_TICKS=2400 (40s) is too short for chase corridors. domt5_1 is ~3300 units start-to-finish; even at walking speed without interference, traversal takes ~110 seconds. Combined with the recovery thrashing burning 12 recovery phases (MAX_RECOVERY_PHASES=12) in under 30s, drive times out / Stuck. Then chase auto-pilot disengages, immediately re-engages, repeats. Aaron sees: party stuck in place, footsteps continuous, no progress. Fixes: (Fix A) Save `float origDx = dx; float origDz = dz;` right after dx/dz are computed from tx/tz (the long-range target). Use origDx/origDz for the heading bitmask threshold check instead of the post-corridor-override dx/dz. The keyboard wake-up trigger doesn't need to be pixel-precise -- it just needs to push in the right broad direction. Analog steering continues to use the corridor-tuned dx/dz for fine waypoint targeting. Net effect: keyboard and analog AGREE on direction, FF8 reads consistent input, party walks at calibration-pace (estimated ~250 units/sec walking based on calibration phase data). (Fix B) Gate the entire stuck-detection-and-recovery block on `!s_chaseDriveActive`. Add `&& !s_chaseDriveActive` to the `else if (s_driveStuckTicks >= DRIVE_STUCK_THRESH)` condition. Chase corridors are hand-picked and don't need recovery's wiggle nudges; with Fix A in place, main steering can hold a coherent direction without recovery thrashing it. Recovery's perpendicular-nudge logic is fundamentally wrong for elongated triangles on rotated cameras, and disabling it for chase-drive is safer than trying to fix the perp-selection logic in general. The outer chase auto-pilot's per-tick IsChaseDriveActive() check provides the only sensible cancel path (drive eventually arrives or times out). (Fix C) Extended timeout for chase-drive: `int driveMaxTicks = s_chaseDriveActive ? 12000 : DRIVE_MAX_TICKS;` then check `>= driveMaxTicks` instead of `>= DRIVE_MAX_TICKS`. 12000 ticks = 200 seconds; gives enough slack for full corridor traversal at walking pace without the outer loop having to disengage and re-engage. F9 path-finding stays at 40s. Files changed: src/field_nav_autodrive.inl (~50 lines: origDx/origDz save, heading-bitmask using origDx/origDz, extended timeout, recovery gate, three rationale comment blocks). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md, DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Risk: low. (a) origDx/origDz heading change only affects the keyboard bitmask, which is a wake-up trigger; the analog (which determines actual movement direction in the engine) is unchanged. F9's existing NPC-target use case uses long-range steer too, so origDx/origDz == dx/dz in that path and behavior is identical. (b) Recovery gate adds one boolean check; F9 path-finding's behavior is bitwise-identical. (c) Timeout extension is gated on chase-drive flag; F9 sees DRIVE_MAX_TICKS unchanged. Predicted v0.15.9.2.2 BAT outcomes: SUCCESS = party walks south through domt5_1 corridor at full walking pace, reaches the cluster[2] target (382,235) or transitions through a south exit gateway, chase auto-pilot disengages cleanly with 'chase-drive completed' log, chase ends naturally with CHASE-END SUMMARY mode=auto. PARTIAL: party makes real progress (positions visible in periodic [drive] logs each second) but doesn't reach target -- target refinement needed for v0.15.9.2.3 (cluster[2] center may be off-walkmesh or wrong end of corridor). FAIL: party still stuck -- means the corridor steering itself produces a nonsense steer target, or the engine's input-reading is broken in a way we haven't diagnosed; debug further with more periodic-log instrumentation. Most likely actual outcome: SUCCESS or PARTIAL, since the heading-bitmask fix alone should resolve the keyboard-vs-analog conflict that was strangling movement.
  // v0.15.9.2.1: Fix two bugs in v0.15.9.2 chase-drive exposed by 2026-05-10 15:48-15:52 BAT (PARTIAL FAIL). v0.15.9.2 BAT: build clean, chase ASK worked, chase-drive STARTED on domt5_1 with mode=TARGET tgt=(382,235), A* found a 31-triangle 28-waypoint path (startDist=3387), calibration completed cleanly (camRight=(0.041,0.999) camDown=(0.000,-1.000)). Then immediately after calibration F9's UpdateAutoDrive fell through to its entity-catalog check, read s_catalog[s_selectedCatalogIdx] (stale state from before chase started), saw the catalog entity's entityIdx == s_playerEntityIdx, and called StopAutoDrive("No target.") -- which spoke "no target" via SAPI to Aaron. Two coordinated bugs: chase-drive bypass in entity-validation + target-coord lookup + wiggle re-path; chase_auto_pilot disengage detection via IsChaseDriveActive returning s_chaseDriveActive && s_driveActive; silent internal stops via SAPI announce gated on !s_chaseDriveActive; StartChaseDrive caches target coords in s_chaseDriveTargetX/Y. v0.15.9.2.1 build then had include-order issue (autodrive.inl included before directiondrive.inl, couldn't see chase-drive statics); fixed by hoisting four state vars into field_navigation.cpp before the autodrive.inl include. Final state: build clean at 16:25:39, BAT ran 16:32-16:33, party engaged on domt5_1 but stuck thrashing near spawn for 20 seconds. v0.15.9.2.2 (above) addresses the three remaining issues identified from the BAT field log.
  // v0.15.9.2: Pivot chase auto-pilot from constant-direction analog to F9-style path-finding for fields with bends. v0.15.9.1.1 BAT (2026-05-10 15:10-15:15) was a PARTIAL SUCCESS: build clean, cosmetic tick=60 fix worked, party walked one cycle from (-877,2531) to (-769,2217) in domt5_1 (correct south direction, slightly more motion than v0.15.9.1's first cycle). But position then froze at exactly (-769,2217) for 30+ seconds. Both v0.15.9.1 and v0.15.9.1.1 BATs converged on the same end coordinates from different starting positions -- strong signal that the freeze is geometry-driven, not input-driven. Aaron confirmed during BAT review: 'I did not hear the spider approach.' Mountain-shake catch ruled out (no kani audio = no AI-rule trigger). Walkmesh wall confirmed: domt5_1's corridor angles east of pure south, and constant-direction analog can't navigate the bend. v0.15.9.1.1's keep-alive theory was wrong; the engine isn't dropping movement intent, the party physically can't proceed past (-769,2217) without changing heading. The fix is to use FF8's existing path-finding infrastructure (F9 backslash auto-drive). v0.15.9.2 implementation: NEW PUBLIC API in FieldNavigation: StartChaseDrive(int32_t targetX, int32_t targetY, bool walk) + StopChaseDrive() + IsChaseDriveActive(). Lives in field_nav_directiondrive.inl alongside the existing direction-drive API since both share the fake-gamepad infrastructure. StartChaseDrive duplicates the relevant subset of F9's start logic from field_nav_handlekeys.inl: drive state init (s_driveActive, s_driveLastTriId from player's current triangle, stuck/wiggle/ticks counters), calibration setup (piggybacks on s_calibPending so a single calibration covers both F9 and chase-drive), fake gamepad install, default arrive distance, A*+funnel path computation via FindNearestTriangle/AreTrianglesConnected/ComputeAStarPath/FunnelPath, pre-skip waypoints, island-bridging via trigger lines for the rare cross-island case. Skips entity-specific setup (talkRadius, save/draw walk-into, trigger crossing) since chase auto-pilot has no entity to target -- just hardcoded coords. Does NOT skip the line-of-sight first-pass; chase fields use full A*. Walk modifier (W/scancode 0x11) held independently via InjectKey for the duration of the drive (F9 always runs, chase fields like domt5_1 require walking). StopChaseDrive releases W if held, then calls StopAutoDrive(nullptr) to reuse F9's teardown (releases arrows, deactivates analog, removes fake gamepad, clears drive state). nullptr reason suppresses 'Cancelled.' SAPI announce -- chase-drive is silent. Mutex with F9 path-finding: StartChaseDrive refuses if s_driveActive is true and s_chaseDriveActive is false (i.e., F9 is running). F9 backslash handler in field_nav_handlekeys.inl now refuses to start (or cancel) when s_chaseDriveActive is true with announce 'Auto-drive unavailable: chase auto-pilot is active.' Arrow-key cancel branch suppressed for chase-drive (player can't accidentally bump out of chase mode). Mutex with direction-drive: StartChaseDrive refuses if s_directionDriveActive (chase auto-pilot routes to one or the other per-field via its config). chase_auto_pilot rewired with per-field mode discriminator: NEW enum FieldDriveMode { MODE_DIRECTION, MODE_TARGET }. FieldConfig extended to {fieldName, mode, dirX, dirY, targetX, targetY, walk}. domt4_1 stays MODE_DIRECTION (RUN LEFT, dirX=-1 dirY=0) -- v0.15.9.1 BAT proved direction-drive cleared this field in one walking step; geometry is short. domt5_1 switches to MODE_TARGET with target (382, 235) -- the cluster[2] center from v0.15.9.1.1 BAT's walkmesh dead-end scanner output (14 narrow tris in the south of the walkmesh, the largest non-northern cluster, likely the field-exit corridor entry). DirectionName() helper updated to handle (0,0) case as 'target'. Engage() routes to StartDirectionDrive or StartChaseDrive per cfg->mode. Disengage() routes to StopDirectionDrive or StopChaseDrive per s_engagedMode (cached on engage). Per-tick refresh in Update's already-engaged-on-this-field branch: MODE_DIRECTION re-calls StartDirectionDrive (idempotent + keep-alive pulse cycle from v0.15.9.1.1); MODE_TARGET checks IsChaseDriveActive() and Disengages with 'chase-drive completed (target reached or stuck)' if false (path-finder finished but no field transition fired -- means target was wrong, refine in v0.15.9.3). Per-second diagnostic split per mode: DIRECTION logs 'mode=DIRECTION dir=(dX,dY) walk=B pos=(pX,pY) lX=N lY=M' (lX/lY meaningful as constant analog vote); TARGET logs 'mode=TARGET tgt=(tX,tY) walk=B pos=(pX,pY) dist=(dX,dY)' (lX/lY would be noise since path-finding steers per-tick). Initialize log line updated. Files changed: src/field_navigation.h (~32 lines: 3 new public-API decls + comment block documenting the chase-drive design and F9 mutex). src/field_nav_directiondrive.inl (~280 lines: chase-drive design rationale, file-scope state s_chaseDriveActive + s_chaseDriveWalk, StartChaseDrive / StopChaseDrive / IsChaseDriveActive at namespace scope, version comment bumped). src/field_nav_handlekeys.inl (~15 lines: arrow-cancel suppression when s_chaseDriveActive, F9 toggle refusal with announce). src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (~150 lines net: header trail extended with v0.15.9.2 design rationale, FieldDriveMode enum, FieldConfig refactored with mode discriminator + targetX/Y, kFieldConfigs updated with per-field mode picks, s_engagedMode + s_engagedTargetX/Y cached state, DirectionName extended for (0,0)='target', Engage routes per mode, Disengage routes per mode, Initialize/Update logs reflect mode). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Risk: moderate. The chase-drive duplicates ~150 lines of F9's start logic to avoid refactoring F9 itself (lower regression risk for general nav). The shared s_driveActive flag means F9's UpdateAutoDrive state machine runs whenever EITHER F9 or chase-drive is active; same path-finding logic, just different entry points. The s_chaseDriveActive flag distinguishes them for mutex purposes. Edge cases: (1) if chase auto-pilot's target is unreachable (different walkmesh island, no bridge trigger), StartChaseDrive's island-bridging logic kicks in -- mirrors F9 verbatim. (2) if the player presses backslash to cancel during a chase, the F9 handler's new gate refuses with announce -- cleaner than silently ignoring. (3) if the player taps an arrow during chase, the new arrow-cancel gate suppresses cancel -- chase auto-pilot stays engaged. (4) if F9 is already running when chase-drive tries to start, StartChaseDrive returns false; chase_auto_pilot retries on next Update tick when F9 might have stopped. Predicted v0.15.9.2 BAT outcomes: SUCCESS = same chase trigger flow, party engages on domt4_1 with MODE_DIRECTION RUN LEFT (clears field per v0.15.9.1 BAT pattern), engages on domt3_2 if Aaron drives manually through it (still unconfigured), engages on domt5_1 with MODE_TARGET (382,235) walk=true. F9 path-finder computes A*+funnel route from spawn position around the wall at (-769,2217) and continues to the south corridor. Per-second diagnostic shows pos changing each second as path-finding steers. Field transitions when party reaches the south exit gateway. Chase ends naturally with CHASE-END SUMMARY mode=auto. PARTIAL: domt5_1 path computed but party gets stuck before reaching target. F9's wiggle-recovery state machine handles most stuck cases; if it gives up, IsChaseDriveActive() returns false and we Disengage with the 'completed (target reached or stuck)' log line. Final position visible in diagnostic; tells us which target refinement is needed for v0.15.9.3. FAIL: F9 path-finding regressed in some way. Most likely cause: the chase-drive's shared use of s_driveActive collides with F9's expectations. Mitigation: I duplicated all F9 state init verbatim, so this should be safe; F9 backslash test path still available for verification. domt5_1 target choice reasoning: cluster[2] center=(382,235) from v0.15.9.1.1 BAT walkmesh data is the largest narrow cluster in the south of the walkmesh (14 tris). Player spawns near (-877,2531), gets stuck at (-769,2217). cluster[2] at X=382 is significantly EAST of the stuck point (-769) -- i.e., the corridor turns east before heading south. Path-finder will compute the southeast-then-south route. If (382,235) is off-walkmesh, FindNearestTriangle snaps to the nearest triangle. After v0.15.9.2 BAT SUCCESS, v0.15.9.3 could fill in domt3_2 and refine targets; v0.15.9.4+ ships the doopen2a bridge state machine (PJUMPA hook + Aaron's AI rule #2).
  // v0.15.9.1.1: Add keep-alive pulse to FieldNavigation's direction-drive path so the chase auto-pilot's continuous southward analog input doesn't get debounced by FF8's apparent ~60-tick walking-cycle movement-intent gate. v0.15.9.1 BAT was a partial success: build clean, chase ASK fully worked (Aaron committed Auto at 14:38:10, INI persisted), field transitions on domt4_1 and domt3_2 happened, ChaseAutoPilot ENGAGED on domt5_1 with WALK SOUTH. Party MOVED on domt5_1: pos went (-843,2482) -> (-769,2217) over the first second, then froze for 80+ seconds. Theory was engine debounce -- BAT showed theory was wrong (v0.15.9.1.1 had same end position as v0.15.9.1, so freeze is geometry not debounce). Keep-alive pulse stays in place as a marginal helper for any future direction-drive use; cosmetic tick=60 fix shipped here. v0.15.9.2 supersedes the freeze fix with path-finding.
  // v0.15.9.1: Wire chase auto-pilot into FieldNavigation's analog-override path. v0.15.9 BAT (2026-05-10 13:19-13:24) was a partial fail. Build deployed cleanly; chase ASK opened with descriptive labels at 13:22:33; cursor announces fired correctly across the 13:22:43-52 navigation window; Aaron committed Auto at 13:22:55 (commit reason=state left 0xD capturing answer=2); INI persistence wrote chase_mode=auto. ChaseAutoPilot ENGAGED on domt5_1 at 13:23:18 with direction=south WALKING (arrows=0x2 holdCancel=1) -- the framework wiring fired correctly. But two things broke. (a) The auto-pilot MISSED domt4_1, the chase START field. The chase ASK was open from 13:22:33 (deferred-open) to ~13:23:10 (slot 2 still rendering). The v0.15.9 four-condition engagement gate's #4 condition (!ChaseAskOverlay::IsAskActive()) kept chase_auto_pilot disengaged the entire ~37s the ASK was open. By the time the ASK fully cleared, Aaron had already transitioned to MH-3 and then MH-7; auto-pilot never got a chance to fire on the start field. (b) Once engaged on domt5_1 at 13:23:18, the party didn't move. Between ENGAGED and Aaron quitting at 13:24:04 (~46 seconds elapsed), no field transition occurred. NO CHASE-END SUMMARY line fired -- chase never ended naturally. Aaron confirmed during BAT review: 'I did not hear the party move at all when the ASK dialog was visible.' For the post-ASK 46-second window on MH-7 he doesn't recall whether there was footstep audio. ROOT CAUSE per field_nav_autodrive.inl's v05.85 comment: 'Keyboard injection is REQUIRED to activate the game's movement code path. Analog steering overrides the direction, but keyboard buttons are the trigger that makes the game process movement at all.' chase_auto_pilot's v0.15.9 standalone SendInput arrows + W weren't enough. The engine reads movement direction from the gamepad analog stick (DIJOYSTATE2 lX/lY); keyboard is just a wake-up trigger. F9 path-finding works because StartAutoDrive installs a fake gamepad and writes lX/lY values; chase_auto_pilot did neither. v0.15.9.1 ships THREE coordinated fixes plus per-second diagnostic logging. (1) DROP THE !IsAskActive() GATE in chase_auto_pilot.cpp::Update. FF8 already blocks input during ASK regardless, so the gate added no protection but did delay engagement on chase-START fields by tens of seconds. With the gate removed, auto-pilot engages immediately on field entry; the engine queues our analog values until the ASK closes, then movement begins. Engagement gate is now THREE conditions (was four): IsInChaseField + GetChaseMode==MODE_AUTO + IsOnField. (2) NEW PUBLIC API in FieldNavigation: StartDirectionDrive(int8_t dirX, int8_t dirY, bool walk) + StopDirectionDrive() + IsDirectionDriveActive(). dirX/dirY are screen-relative direction signs in {-1, 0, +1} matching DirectInput axis convention (dirX +1 = right, dirY +1 = south). New module field_nav_directiondrive.inl (~190 lines): installs the fake gamepad (mirrors the install code in field_nav_handlekeys.inl's F9 drive branch, idempotent), activates s_analogOverrideActive, writes screen-relative analog values directly (lX = dirX * 1000, lY = dirY * 1000, NO camera projection), holds one keyboard arrow as wake-up trigger via the existing SetHeldDirections helper, and (if walk=true) holds W (scancode 0x11) via SendInput. Mutex with F9 path-finding via new s_directionDriveActive flag: StartDirectionDrive refuses if s_driveActive is true; F9 handler in field_nav_handlekeys.inl refuses if s_directionDriveActive is true. The two paths share infrastructure but cannot run concurrently. .inl is included in field_navigation.cpp AFTER field_nav_autodrive.inl (so SetHeldDirections / InjectKey / ReleaseAllDirections are visible) and BEFORE field_nav_handlekeys.inl (so the F9 handler sees the new flag). (3) chase_auto_pilot REWIRED to use the new API. Replaces the v0.15.9 standalone SendInput infrastructure (InjectKey / ReleaseAll / SetHeld helpers, scan code constants, DIR_* bitmask) with FieldNavigation::StartDirectionDrive / StopDirectionDrive calls. FieldConfig struct refactored from {fieldName, arrowMask, holdCancel} to {fieldName, dirX, dirY, walk} -- maps more directly to the DirectInput convention. Same two configured fields: domt4_1 RUN LEFT = (-1, 0, false); domt5_1 WALK SOUTH = (0, +1, true). chase_ask_overlay.h include dropped (no longer needed -- the gate it satisfied is gone). PER-SECOND DIAGNOSTIC LOG. While engaged, chase_auto_pilot::Update logs once per ~60 ticks: 'ChaseAutoPilot: tick=T field=X dir=(dX,dY) walk=B pos=(pX,pY) lX=N lY=M'. Position reads SEH-guarded from entity[0] (Squall) +0x190/+0x194 (matching field_nav_helpers.inl::GetEntityPos's fixed-point path). If pX/pY change tick-to-tick, movement IS reaching the engine; if frozen, fake gamepad install isn't taking effect. lX/lY values are computed from the engaged field's cfg -- if they ever look wrong vs the dir field, the FieldConfig table has a bug. v0.15.9.1 BAT plan: same chase trigger as v0.15.9 except Aaron should keep playing through MH-7 even if it feels like nothing's happening for a few seconds (the engine needs ~60 ticks to engage movement after fake gamepad install per field_nav_autodrive.inl's v06.08 comment). Watch for the per-second diagnostic log line. v0.15.9.1 BAT outcomes: SUCCESS = party walks south on domt5_1, transitions to next field, chase eventually ends with CHASE-END SUMMARY mode=auto battles_fired=0 battles_suppressed=N -> push v0.15.9.1, move to v0.15.9.2 (bridge state machine, fill in domt3_2 direction). PARTIAL: party still doesn't move on domt5_1 -- the diagnostic log tells us why. Two likely sub-cases: (a) position frozen, lX/lY correct -> fake gamepad install isn't taking effect; re-check DD_InstallFakeGamepad ordering vs FF8's gamepad init. (b) position moves but never reaches exit -> direction wrong for this field's camera, or stuck on geometry; try other directions, check walkmesh. FAIL: F9 path-finding broke -> refactor regression in field_nav_directiondrive.inl or its mutex hook in field_nav_handlekeys.inl. Roll back FieldNavigation changes; ship v0.15.9.1.1 with just Fix 1 + diagnostic logging, leave Fix 2 for v0.15.9.2. Files changed: src/field_navigation.h (~30 lines: 3 new public-API decls in namespace, comment block documenting screen-relative axis convention + mutex with F9). src/field_nav_directiondrive.inl (NEW, ~190 lines: design rationale, file-scope state s_directionDriveActive + s_directionDriveWalk + SC_W_CANCEL_DD, DD_InstallFakeGamepad / DD_UninstallFakeGamepad / DD_DirsToArrowMask helpers, StartDirectionDrive / StopDirectionDrive / IsDirectionDriveActive at namespace scope). src/field_navigation.cpp (~6 lines: include the new .inl between autodrive.inl and gps.inl). src/field_nav_handlekeys.inl (~7 lines: F9 mutex check refuses if s_directionDriveActive). src/chase_auto_pilot.h (~30 lines refactored: design rationale updated for v0.15.9.1, three-condition engagement gate documented, diagnostic logging documented). src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (rewritten ~210 lines: dropped <windows.h> SendInput infrastructure, dropped chase_ask_overlay.h include, FieldConfig struct refactored to {fieldName, dirX, dirY, walk}, ReadSquallPosition SEH-guarded helper, Engage now calls FieldNavigation::StartDirectionDrive, Disengage calls StopDirectionDrive, three-condition engagement gate, per-second diagnostic block in Update). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Risk: low-to-moderate. The new direction-drive API is purely additive; it cannot affect F9 path-finding when no one calls Start/Stop. The mutex check in F9 is a single boolean condition that fires only when chase auto-pilot is active. The chase_auto_pilot rewrite preserves the v0.15.9 engagement logic (just drops one gate condition + swaps the input mechanism). Edge case: if field_nav_directiondrive.inl's DD_InstallFakeGamepad runs while F9's StartAutoDrive happens to be partway through its install block (impossible in the single-threaded mod-loop model but worth flagging), s_savedDevicePtr would get clobbered. Mitigation: both paths run on the same Update tick, never interleave; the mutex check makes this impossible by design. v0.15.9 BAT findings preserved: chase route is domt4_1 -> domt3_2 -> domt5_1 -> ... -> doopen2a; domt3_2 still unconfigured in v0.15.9.1, will be filled in v0.15.9.2+ once we know which direction works.
  // v0.15.9: Auto chase mode (X-ATM092 auto-flee) framework + linear-field auto-drive + walk-not-run on west trail. Builds on v0.15.8.1's clean chase ASK UX (descriptive labels, suppressed redundant commit announce). Three coordinated changes route Auto from "falls back to manual" to actual auto-drive behavior. (1) chase_ask_overlay::CommitChoice ANSWER_AUTO branch now calls ChaseDetector::SetChaseMode(MODE_AUTO) instead of MODE_MANUAL fallback. ANSWER_ORIGINAL still falls back to MODE_MANUAL until v0.15.10 ships the chase-mod-active flag (vanilla chase, no battle cap). (2) chase_battle_freeze::Hook_opcode_battle gate reworked into a per-mode cap: int cap = (mode == MODE_AUTO) ? 0 : 1; bool freeze = (battleCount >= cap). MANUAL keeps cap=1 (first scripted chase battle PASSes, subsequent NO-OP) so the v0.15.8.1 BAT-proven chase-cap behavior is preserved verbatim. AUTO uses cap=0 -- ALL chase battles NO-OP'd, including the scripted opener in domt4_1. The chase progresses as a movement-only scenario: chase_auto_pilot drives, robot scripts play visually, the BATTLE opcode never reaches a battle screen. The doopen2a strcmp guard in the PASS branch is unchanged (still skips RegisterChaseAgent for the bridge's director0 entity). Cap=0 doubles as the safety net for the AI-rule fields: if chase_auto_pilot's W-press hasn't engaged in time on the west trail (race window at field entry), the ground-shake battle that would fire from running gets NO-OP'd anyway. Log lines updated: NO-OP and PASS lines now print 'cap=N' so the post-BAT log shows which mode was active for each chase battle event. Initialize banner updated: 'v0.15.9 (... cap=1 in MANUAL ... cap=0 in AUTO ...)'. (3) NEW MODULE src/chase_auto_pilot.{h,cpp} (~280 lines total). On chase field entry with mode=AUTO, looks up the field name in a hardcoded FieldConfig table and (if found) injects raw keyboard input via SendInput hardware scan codes -- same pattern as field_nav_autodrive.inl::InjectKey, no analog stick override since chase fields are linear corridors. The walkmesh constrains lateral drift; the chase exit gateway acts as the natural terminator (field transition fires on contact). Per-field config: arrowMask (DIR_UP/DIR_DOWN/DIR_LEFT/DIR_RIGHT bitmask) + holdCancel (true = hold W to walk). Initial config covers two fields with known behavior: domt4_1 (Mountain Hideout 6, chase start / Selphie cliff) RUN LEFT per Jegged.com 'run immediately to the left as quickly as possible. If you delay at all, you will have to fight X-ATM092 again'; domt5_1 (Mountain Hideout 7, west trail) WALK DOWN per Jegged 'pathway leading south. Walk, don't run, to the bottom of the screen' AND Aaron's AI rule #1 confirmed 2026-05-10 (mountain shakes when running, party loses balance, gets caught -- AND walk modifier is W key, scancode 0x11, FF8 PC default cancel binding which on foot acts as walk modifier opposite of FF7's run modifier). Other chase fields (domt1_1, domt2_1, domt3_2, doopen2a) are unconfigured -- ChaseAutoPilot::Update returns silently for unrecognized field names, player drives manually. doopen2a (bridge) handling deferred to v0.15.9.1 with PJUMPA hook detecting kani's two scripted leaps over the party + reverse-direction state machine per Aaron's AI rule #2. Engagement gate (all four must hold): IsInChaseField(), GetChaseMode()==MODE_AUTO, IsOnField() (game mode 1, not battle/menu/dialog), !ChaseAskOverlay::IsAskActive() (defensive). Disengagement releases all held keys (arrows + W) cleanly via SendInput KEYUP events. Diff-based SetHeld function reconciles held-key state to a desired tuple, idempotent if state already matches -- defends against rare cases where Windows drops a held key (focus-loss races); cheap, called every Update tick on engaged field. ChaseAutoPilot wired into dinput8.cpp's Initialize/Update/Shutdown sequence between ChaseAskOverlay and ChaseKaniFreeze. New compile-list entry in src/deploy.bat after chase_ask_overlay.cpp. Predicted v0.15.9 BAT outcome (Aaron drives FF8 to chase trigger, picks Auto in dialog, then goes hands-off): cursor announces 'Auto: falls back to manual selected' (dialog text unchanged from v0.15.8.1; the description label still says 'falls back to manual' until we update kChaseChoices in a later version once v0.15.9.1 ships). Aaron presses X to commit. SAPI says 'Automatic selected'; ChaseDetector logs MODE_AUTO. Chase resumes; chase_auto_pilot picks up on next Update where IsInChaseField+IsOnField+GetChaseMode==AUTO+!IsAskActive all hold. Logs show 'ChaseAutoPilot: ENGAGED on field="domt4_1" direction=west running (arrows=0x4 holdCancel=0)'. Party walks left, no chase battle fires (cap=0 NO-OP); field log shows '[CBF] NO-OP chase BATTLE call ... mode=auto cap=0' once for the would-be opener. Field transitions when party reaches gateway. ENGAGED line for the next field (likely an unconfigured one) doesn't fire; Aaron drives manually until reaching domt5_1. ENGAGED line for domt5_1 fires with 'direction=south WALKING (arrows=0x2 holdCancel=1)'; party walks south slowly with W held; no mountain-shake battle (the hypothetical battle is NO-OP'd anyway by cap=0). Continued progress through unconfigured fields (player drives) until reaching doopen2a. ENGAGED doesn't fire for doopen2a; Aaron handles the bridge manually (turns around twice as the robot leaps). doopen2a -> dotown_3 transition fires per v0.15.2.15's preserved fieldId-flip mechanic; Lapin Beach FMV plays through; control returns to dotown_2. Total chase battles fought: zero. Total chase battles NO-OP'd: ~5-7 depending on which fields the auto-pilot didn't reach in time. Risk: low. The chase_auto_pilot module is purely additive -- it does nothing when mode!=AUTO, so MANUAL chases work exactly as v0.15.8.1. The cap=0 in AUTO is a single-line gate change that preserves the entire MANUAL path verbatim. The keyboard injection uses SendInput which is a documented Win32 API the field_nav_autodrive.inl path has been exercising successfully since v0.05.85; same scancode (W=0x11) as v0.14.102's car AD path. The W modifier might briefly skip a stray dialog if one fires while engaged on domt5_1 (cancel acts as 'next' in dialogs); chase fields rarely have mid-field dialogs so risk is low; if it surfaces in BAT, v0.15.9.0.1 can add a !FieldDialog::IsDialogActive() gate. Files changed: src/chase_auto_pilot.h (NEW, ~75 lines: design rationale, public API). src/chase_auto_pilot.cpp (NEW, ~210 lines: scan code constants, FieldConfig table, InjectKey/ReleaseAll/SetHeld diff-based state machine, LookupConfig, Engage/Disengage with field-channel logging, Initialize/Update/Shutdown). src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (~15 lines: ANSWER_AUTO branch routes to MODE_AUTO with rationale comment; ANSWER_ORIGINAL retains MODE_MANUAL fallback with comment trail to v0.15.10). src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp (~30 lines: cap variable + per-mode logic, NO-OP and PASS log lines updated to print cap=N, Initialize banner updated to v0.15.9 wording). src/dinput8.cpp (4 spots: include chase_auto_pilot.h, ChaseAutoPilot::Initialize after ChaseAskOverlay::Initialize, ChaseAutoPilot::Update after ChaseAskOverlay::Update in main loop, ChaseAutoPilot::Shutdown between ChaseBattleFreeze and ChaseKaniFreeze). src/deploy.bat (1 line: chase_auto_pilot.cpp added to compile list after chase_ask_overlay.cpp). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. After v0.15.9 BAT SUCCESS, v0.15.9.1 implements the doopen2a bridge state machine. After v0.15.9.1, the kChaseChoices labels in chase_ask_overlay can be updated from 'Auto: falls back to manual' to 'Auto: hands-off chase' or similar.
  // v0.15.8.1: Chase ASK menu UX polish per v0.15.8 BAT feedback. v0.15.8 BAT was a clean SUCCESS (dialog opened on chase trigger, cursor announced through all three options, X commit captured Manual at 11:05:06, chase resumed with 1-battle-per-field cap holding through Selphie's rendezvous line) but Aaron noticed a glitch: the chase resumed while three TTS announces were still queued post-commit (1) cursor highlight 'Manual selected', (2) DialogInject's 'You chose Manual', (3) chase_ask_overlay's verbose 'Manual mode selected. One battle per chase field will be allowed.' Combined ~10 seconds of speech vs the chase's lack of patience for TTS to drain. v0.15.8.1 fix: (1) Choice labels in chase_ask_overlay now include brief descriptions ('Manual: one battle per field' / 'Auto: falls back to manual' / 'Original: falls back to manual') so the description is spoken naturally on cursor change during navigation rather than as a separate post-commit announce. (2) DialogInject::OpenAsk gains a new parameter announceCommit (default true so Phase2_TestAsk is unchanged). When false, Update()'s commit branch suppresses the 'You chose <name>' SAPI announce (still logs '[DLG-INJ] v0.15.8.1 commit announce suppressed by caller'). chase_ask_overlay passes false; Phase2_TestAsk passes true. (3) chase_ask_overlay's CommitChoice trimmed from verbose multi-clause messages to single brief lines: 'Manual selected' / 'Automatic selected' / 'Original selected' per Aaron's exact phrasing. ChaseDetector::SetChaseMode(MODE_MANUAL) routing unchanged for all three options. (4) Infrastructure: PHASE2_NAME_CAP bumped 32 -> 64 to fit descriptive labels (8 * 64 = 512 bytes static, was 256, harmless). Cursor-announce msg buffer bumped 64 -> 128 in two spots (cursor-change announcer + commit announcer) so longer names + ' selected' / 'You chose ' suffixes don't snprintf-truncate. New static s_phase2AnnounceCommit set per OpenAskInternal call from caller's announceCommit param. Predicted v0.15.8.1 BAT outcome: trigger chase. After ~3s deferred-open, dialog renders, FieldDialog [ASK] hook speaks 'Mode?. Selected: Manual: one battle per field. Auto: falls back to manual. Original: falls back to manual.' followed by initial cursor announce 'Manual: one battle per field selected'. Press Down -- 'Auto: falls back to manual selected'. Press Down -- 'Original: falls back to manual selected'. Press X to commit on any -- single brief 'Manual selected' (or 'Automatic selected' / 'Original selected'). NO 'You chose <name>' announce (suppressed). Chase resumes promptly. ChaseDetector logs the mode change. Subsequent chase battles cap at 1 per field as in v0.15.8. Files changed: src/dialog_inject.h (~10 lines: announceCommit param added with rationale comment, default true). src/dialog_inject.cpp (~30 lines: PHASE2_NAME_CAP bump, msg buffer bumps, s_phase2AnnounceCommit state var, OpenAskInternal new param, gating in commit branch, log line update at arm time, Phase2_TestAsk/OpenAsk pass-through, v0.15.8.1 comment trail). src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (~25 lines: kChaseChoices descriptive strings, OpenAsk announceCommit=false call, CommitChoice trimmed to brief announces, v0.15.8.1 comments). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Risk: very low. Description-strings change is data-only; only ASCII punctuation that the EncodeFf8 table covers (colon 0x2D, period 0x3B, space 0x20, letters) so no new encoder work. announceCommit gating is a single boolean check; default-true preserves Phase2_TestAsk's existing diagnostic behavior. Buffer size bumps are safe (snprintf truncates; we're enlarging not shrinking). chase_ask_overlay's MODE_MANUAL routing unchanged so the proven v0.15.8 chase-cap behavior is preserved verbatim. Edge case: if the engine's dialog box has visual layout limits per option line, descriptive labels (~30 chars each) might overflow on screen visually -- not a functional concern (Aaron is blind) and the [ASK] hook reads the encoded buffer regardless of visual rendering, so TTS path is unaffected. After v0.15.8.1 BAT SUCCESS, v0.15.9 reclaims its planned slot for Auto = run-from-robot; v0.15.10 for Original = chase-mod-active flag. The Auto/Original descriptions in kChaseChoices update at those points to reflect implemented behavior.
  // v0.15.8: Wire chase_ask_overlay into DialogInject's OpenAsk pipeline. v0.15.7.1 BAT proved end-to-end: dialog opens via engine, cursor input announces, commit captured on X. Now chase_ask_overlay calls DialogInject::OpenAsk('Mode?', {'Manual','Auto','Original'}, ...) instead of running its v0.15.2.2 TTS-only fallback. New public API in dialog_inject: OpenAsk(prompt, choices, numChoices, defaultCursor, slot) and ResetLastAnswer(). Internal refactor: Phase2_TestAsk's body factored into OpenAskInternal which both Phase2_TestAsk (hardcoded 'Mode?/Manual/Auto/Original' on slot 2) and the public OpenAsk call. CurQToOptionName replaced with a lookup into s_phase2ChoiceNames (caller-supplied) so the cursor-change announcer speaks any provided choice. chase_ask_overlay rewire: removes proxy-slot scaffolding (PopulateProxySlot, ReleaseProxySlot, LogProxySlotSnapshot, SyncEngineCursor, the FF8 EncodeChar table, slot template constants) and the manual VK_UP/VK_DOWN/VK_RETURN polling. Replaces with a thin wrapper: OnDialogText defers 3 seconds (so Squall's 'Forget it!' line plays first), then OpenAsk calls DialogInject::OpenAsk and sets s_askOpen. Update polls DialogInject::GetLastAnswer per frame; on non--1 it dispatches via CommitChoice which announces and routes to ChaseDetector::SetChaseMode(MODE_MANUAL) regardless of which option was picked (Auto/Original announce 'not yet implemented, falling back to manual' per Aaron's plan; v0.15.9 will implement Auto = run-from-robot, v0.15.10 will implement Original = chase-mod-active flag). The s_askFiredThisChase once-per-chase gate stays. Predicted v0.15.8 BAT outcome: trigger the chase scene (Squall says 'Forget it!  Let's go!'). After ~3 seconds (TRIGGER_DELAY_MS), hear the engine ASK render: 'Mode?. Selected: Manual. Auto. Original' + 'Manual selected'. Press arrows -- 'Auto selected' / 'Original selected' announce as in v0.15.7.1 BAT. Press X (FF8 confirm) -- hear 'You chose X' announce, then 'X selected. Falling back to manual mode.' (or for Manual: 'Manual mode selected. One battle per chase field will be allowed.'). ChaseDetector::SetChaseMode logs the mode change. Subsequent chase battles cap at 1 per field. Squall and party walk during ASK -- known limitation deferred per Aaron's instruction (v0.15.7.1 trail explains). Files changed: src/dialog_inject.h (~50 lines: v0.15.8 entry in design rationale; OpenAsk + ResetLastAnswer public decls). src/dialog_inject.cpp (~250 lines: v0.15.8 entry; s_phase2ChoiceNames/Count state; CurQToOptionName replaced with array lookup; OpenAskInternal factored from Phase2_TestAsk body taking caller params; Phase2_TestAsk rewritten as 8-line wrapper; public OpenAsk and ResetLastAnswer). src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (full rewrite, 220 lines down from 460): removes the proxy-slot/EncodeChar/keyboard-polling code, calls DialogInject. src/chase_ask_overlay.h (slim public API, comment trail updated). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Risk: medium. The dialog wiring path is proven; risk is in chase_ask_overlay's deferred-trigger interaction with DialogInject's once-per-frame Update timing (ResetLastAnswer is called immediately before OpenAsk so any stale answer is wiped; OpenAsk arms detection only on retCode==1; GetLastAnswer returns -1 until commit). Edge case: if DialogInject::OpenAsk returns false (slot busy or address resolution failed), we don't set s_askFiredThisChase, so a future trigger MES could try again -- but trigger MES only fires once per chase entry naturally so this is mostly defensive. Aaron will not toggle Shift+F12 during BAT; the standalone test path stays in code as a fallback.
  // v0.15.7.1: Premature commit fix from v0.15.7 BAT. v0.15.7 BAT log showed answer-detection firing 'commit reason=state left 0xD capturing answer=1' on the very first poll after arming, before Aaron pressed any key. Sequence: (1) opcode_ask returns 1 with slot 2 trans=0x0000 vel=0x0200 state=0x00000000 (the dialog has begun opening but not yet rendered); (2) Update() arms answer-detection at this exact moment; (3) on next frame, Update() reads state=0x00000000 and curQ=1 -- it announces 'Manual selected' for the cursor change (correct, that's the initial position), THEN tests 'state != 0xD' -- TRUE because state is still 0x00 -- and trips the commit reason='state left 0xD'. The dialog actually progressed 0x00 -> 0x01 -> 0x0D over ~450 ms. Fix: track whether the cursor-active state (0xD with D2 bit set) has been entered at least once, gate the 'state left 0xD' and 'D2 bit clear' commit branches on that flag. The 60-second timeout is still unconditional. v0.15.7.1 BAT-PASSED at t+422ms (predicted ~450ms): cursor announces fired one-per-press across 14 seconds of arrow presses with no premature commit, final answer 3 = Original captured on X press. GetLastAnswer() ready for v0.15.8 chase wiring.
